      "When a person cuts a finger, the body sends cells to the injury, repairs the damaged tissue, and within a few weeks the skin may look almost as it did before. It is natural to assume that trees recover from injuries in a similar way, and for centuries gardeners spoke of trees healing their wounds as bark slowly closed over a broken branch or a gash made by a careless axe. Yet trees cannot replace damaged wood in the way that animals replace damaged tissue. Once the wood inside a trunk has been injured, it stays injured for the rest of the tree's life, and the tree survives not by repairing the damage but by isolating it.",
      "Much of our modern understanding of this process comes from the work of Alex Shigo, a scientist with the United States Forest Service who, from the 1960s onward, examined thousands of trees by cutting them open lengthwise with a chainsaw. Instead of looking only at the outside of wounds, as most earlier researchers had done, he followed the patterns of discolored and decaying wood deep inside the trunk. What he found was that decay did not spread at random. It was confined within boundaries, as if the tree had built walls around the injured area to keep fungi and bacteria from moving into healthy wood.",
      "Shigo described a system of four such walls. The first slows the movement of decay up and down the trunk by plugging the tiny tubes that carry water, the second resists inward spread across the growth rings, and the third limits sideways spread through narrow bands of cells that run from the center of the tree toward the bark. These three walls form a kind of box around the wound, but none of them is perfect, and decay may slowly push through the weaker ones over many years. The fourth wall is different, because it is formed after the injury by the thin layer of living cells beneath the bark, which begins producing new wood with special chemical defenses, separating everything that existed at the time of the wound from everything that grows afterward.",
      "This idea, which Shigo called compartmentalization, explains why a large old tree can stand for decades with a hollow center. The hollow is simply an old, sealed compartment that decay has eaten away, while the tree continues to live and grow in the healthy wood outside the fourth wall. ( A ), a hollow tree is not necessarily a dying tree, and removing such trees merely because they are hollow may destroy individuals that could have lived for many more years, along with the owls, bats, and insects that make their homes inside them.",
      "Shigo's findings also changed the practice of tree care. Earlier generations of tree surgeons often scraped decayed wood out of hollows and filled the cavities with concrete, believing that they were cleaning a wound much as a dentist cleans a tooth. In fact, by cutting into the surrounding wood, they frequently broke through the very walls that had been holding the decay in check. Similarly, the common habit of painting cut branches with tar or other dressings was shown to do little good, and in some cases to trap moisture that encouraged the growth of fungi.",
      "Perhaps the most practical change concerned the way branches are removed. Gardeners had long been taught to cut branches flush with the trunk, leaving a neat, smooth surface that looked tidy and professional. Shigo showed, however, that the slightly swollen ring of tissue at the base of a branch, known as the branch collar, contains strong natural defenses, and that cutting through it opens the trunk itself to decay. Today, most guidelines recommend cutting just outside the collar, leaving a small bump that may look untidy but allows the tree to seal off the wound quickly.",
      "The story of compartmentalization is a reminder that living things do not always solve problems in the ways we expect. A tree, unable to move away from danger or to replace what it has lost, has developed a strategy of containment rather than repair, accepting damage while preventing it from spreading. Understanding that strategy has allowed people to care for trees in ways that work with their natural defenses rather than against them.",
