      "In many towns, fruit trees grow in back gardens, beside old houses, and along quiet roads. Each autumn, these trees produce far more apples, pears, and plums than their owners can possibly eat. Much of this fruit falls to the ground and rots, attracting wasps and making paths slippery for people who walk by. In the small town of Westbrook, a group of residents decided that this waste was a problem they could solve together, and in 2019 they held their first Fruit Rescue Day.",
      "The idea behind the event was simple. Before the day, the organizers asked people with fruit trees to sign up on a website or by telephone. Volunteers were then divided into small teams, and each team was given a list of gardens to visit, along with ladders, long poles with baskets on the end, and plenty of boxes. On the day itself, more than sixty volunteers took part, and by the evening they had collected about nine hundred kilograms of fruit from thirty-two different gardens.",
      "The fruit was shared according to a rule that the organizers had agreed on in advance. One third went to the owner of the tree, one third was taken home by the volunteers, and the last third was given to a local food bank and to two schools. ( A ), everyone involved received something, and nobody felt that they were simply giving away their property or working for free. Several tree owners said they had never received so much of their own fruit in a usable form, because they were usually too busy or too old to climb a ladder.",
      "Not everything went smoothly in the first year. Some of the fruit had already been damaged by insects or birds, and sorting it took much longer than anyone had expected. A few volunteers were nervous about climbing tall trees, so the organizers later added a short safety lesson at the start of the day. They also learned that the food bank could not store large amounts of fresh fruit for very long, so in the second year they asked a local cafe to turn some of the extra apples into juice and jam, which kept for months.",
      "Today the event has grown into a weekend festival that the whole town looks forward to. Children help to wash and sort the fruit, older residents teach traditional ways of making preserves, and a map of the town's fruit trees is updated every year. The organizers say that the most valuable result is not the fruit itself but the new connections between neighbors. People who had lived on the same street for years without speaking now greet each other and share recipes.",
      "Similar events, often called gleaning, exist in many countries, and the word itself comes from the old custom of collecting the grain left in fields after a harvest. The Westbrook volunteers like to say that their town did not need more food, only a better way to notice the food that was already growing around them.",
