    id: 'adv-bike-repair-club',
    title: 'Second Lives for Forgotten Bicycles',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Every spring, hundreds of bicycles are left behind at the parking area of Minato Station in a small coastal city. Some belong to students who have graduated and moved away, while others have simply been forgotten by their owners. The city collects these bicycles, keeps them for a few months in case their owners come back for them, and then usually sends them away to be broken up and sold as scrap metal.",
      "Three years ago, a science teacher at Minato High School named Ms. Sakai decided that this was a terrible waste. She asked the city whether some of the bicycles could be given to the school instead, and after several meetings, the city agreed to the plan. That was how the school's Bicycle Repair Club began. At first it had only four members, and none of them knew much more than how to put air in a tire.",
      "The members learned by doing. A retired man who had run a bicycle shop in the town for forty years offered to visit every Saturday, and he taught them how to fix brakes, replace chains, and straighten bent wheels. Some bicycles took a whole afternoon to repair, and a few could not be saved at all. ( A ), the members became faster and more skillful every month, and by the end of the first year they had repaired more than eighty bicycles. Parts from the bicycles that could not be saved, such as lights, bells, and seats, were used on others, so almost nothing went to waste.",
      "The finished bicycles are given free of charge to people who need them. Many go to students from other countries who have come to study at the local college, and others go to workers who have recently moved to the city and cannot yet afford a car. Every bicycle is checked twice before it leaves the school, and each new owner receives a short lesson on local traffic rules, which the members explain in simple Japanese and English.",
      "The club has also changed its own members. One second-year student said that she used to be too shy to speak in class, but explaining traffic rules to new owners from Vietnam and Nepal gave her confidence. Another member now hopes to become an engineer. Last year, the club received a prize from the city for its work, but the members say that the best reward is something else: seeing a bicycle that was almost thrown away carry someone to school or work every morning.",
    ],
