    id: 'adv-hot-water-bottle-history',
    title: 'The Long Life of the Hot-Water Bottle',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Before most homes had central heating, getting into a cold bed on a winter night could be a very unpleasant experience. For hundreds of years, people in many parts of the world solved this problem in a simple way: they filled a container with hot water and put it under the blankets before going to sleep. In Japan this item is called a yutanpo, and in English it is usually known as a hot-water bottle.",
      "The idea came to Japan from China several hundred years ago. Early Japanese yutanpo were made of pottery, and later many were made of metal, often with a wavy surface on the top. The waves made the thin metal container stronger, and they also gave it a larger surface, so heat could spread to the bed more easily. People wrapped the yutanpo in cloth so that it would not burn their feet, and in the morning they sometimes used the still-warm water to wash their faces.",
      "In Europe, people used a different tool for a long time. A warming pan was a round metal pan with a lid and a long wooden handle. It was filled with hot coals from the fire and moved quickly between the sheets to warm them before someone got into bed. However, warming pans could burn the sheets or even start a fire if they were left in one place for too long. ( A ), many families were happy when rubber hot-water bottles became common in the early twentieth century.",
      "Rubber bottles were soft, light, and safe to hold, and factories could make them in large numbers at low cost. A Croatian inventor, Eduard Penkala, is often said to have designed one of the first modern rubber bottles in 1903. Over the following decades, the hot-water bottle became a familiar part of winter life in Britain and many other countries, and children often had bottles with soft covers in the shape of animals.",
      "When electric blankets and air conditioners spread, many people stopped using hot-water bottles. In recent years, however, they have become popular again in Japan and elsewhere. Because a hot-water bottle uses no electricity during the night, it is a cheap and eco-friendly way to stay warm. Some people also say that its gentle heat helps them fall asleep more easily than a heater that runs all night and makes the air dry. A simple tool that is hundreds of years old is still useful in the modern world.",
    ],
