    id: 'adv-fog-nets',
    title: 'Catching Water from the Fog',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Along parts of the coast of Chile in South America, there are places where almost no rain falls for years at a time. Yet on many mornings, thick clouds of fog roll in from the Pacific Ocean and cover the hills. The fog is made of millions of tiny drops of water, but these drops are so small that they float in the air instead of falling to the ground like rain. For people who need water, it has always been frustrating to see so much of it passing by just out of reach.",
      "For a long time, people living in these dry areas had to bring in drinking water by truck, which was expensive and not always reliable. Then scientists began to test a simple idea. They put up large sheets of fine plastic net on poles across the hillsides, facing the direction from which the wind usually blows. When the fog passes through the net, some of the tiny drops hit the threads and stick to them.",
      "As more and more drops gather on the threads, they join together and become larger drops. Soon they are too heavy to stay on the net, so they run down and fall into a long pipe at the bottom. The pipe then carries the water into a tank, and from there it can be used for drinking, cooking, growing vegetables or giving water to animals. On a foggy day, each square meter of net may collect several liters of water.",
      "The idea was not completely new, ( A ). Some plants and animals have been collecting water from fog for a very long time. In the deserts of southern Africa, for example, a kind of beetle stands on the top of sand hills in the early morning and lifts its back toward the wind. Drops of fog collect on its back and roll down into its mouth, giving it the water it needs to survive.",
      "Today, fog nets are used in several countries, including Morocco, Peru and parts of Africa. They need no electricity and are fairly cheap to build. However, they also have limits. They only work in places where fog comes often, and the nets can be torn by strong winds, so local people must learn to repair them. Projects work best when the community helps plan them and takes care of them, because a net that nobody looks after will soon stop giving water.",
    ],
