    id: 'adv-tsunami-stones',
    title: 'Messages in Stone: Japan\'s Tsunami Markers',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Along the coast of northeastern Japan, there are hundreds of old stone markers standing quietly beside roads, in fields and near shrines. Many of them were put up after great tsunamis, and some are believed to be several hundred years old. Their purpose was simple but important: to pass on the lessons of one disaster to people who would live long after it.",
      "The messages carved on these stones are different from place to place. Some record the date of a tsunami and the number of people who died in it. Others give clear advice, such as telling readers to run to high ground as soon as they feel a strong earthquake. The people who made the stones knew that memories fade, and they hoped that stone would last longer than human memory.",
      "One famous example stands near a small village called Aneyoshi in Iwate Prefecture. After huge tsunamis hit the area in 1896 and 1933, people there set up a stone with a short warning: do not build your homes below this point. The villagers are said to have followed this advice and built their houses on higher land. In 2011, when a great tsunami struck the coast, the water reportedly stopped a short distance below the stone, and the houses above it survived. The old warning had worked.",
      "However, not every stone was so successful. ( A ) the warnings were written in stone, many of them were forgotten over the years. Some stones were moved when new roads were built, some were hidden by trees and plants, and others were written in an old style of Japanese that is hard to read today. As towns grew, people sometimes built new homes in low areas near the sea, because the last big tsunami had happened before they were born.",
      "After the disaster in 2011, interest in the old stones grew again. Researchers and local groups began to make maps that show where the stones stand and what they say. New stones have also been put up in many towns, often with simple words that even small children can understand. In some places, schools take students to visit the stones and talk with older people about what happened in the past.",
      "The tsunami stones remind us that the greatest danger of a rare disaster may be the simple act of forgetting it. A stone cannot run or speak, but it can wait patiently for a hundred years. Whether its message saves lives depends on the people who stop to read it and decide to listen.",
    ],
