    id: 'adv-laguna-dolphin-fishers',
    title: 'When Dolphins Tell Fishers When to Throw',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In the small town of Laguna in southern Brazil, fishers stand in a line in waist-deep water, holding circular nets and staring at the surface of a muddy channel. They cannot see the fish they hope to catch, because the water is far too cloudy. Instead, they are watching for dolphins. When a dolphin suddenly dives or makes a particular rolling movement near the line, the fishers throw their nets at once, and with luck they pull them back full of mullet.",
      "This partnership between people and wild dolphins has attracted the attention of scientists for decades, and local records suggest that it has existed for well over a century. What makes it remarkable is that neither side is trained or fed by the other. The dolphins, a type of bottlenose dolphin, hunt the mullet by driving schools of fish toward the shore, where the line of fishers blocks their escape. The fishers, in turn, rely on the dolphins to tell them exactly when and where the fish are. Mullet are an important source of income for many families in the town, and the fish are especially plentiful in certain seasons, when large schools move along the coast.",
      "For a long time, however, it was not clear whether this was true cooperation or simply two groups of hunters that happened to be in the same place. The question was difficult to answer, ( A ) the key moments happen underwater and last only a few seconds. In a study published in 2023, an international team of researchers used drones to film the scene from above, underwater microphones to record the dolphins' sounds, and GPS devices worn by the fishers to track their movements.",
      "The results suggested that the two sides really do coordinate their behavior. Fishers who threw their nets soon after a dolphin's signal were far more likely to catch fish than those who threw at other times. Such evidence is important, because it shows that the benefit is not merely imagined by the fishers. The recordings also showed that the dolphins changed the way they used sound when they were working near the fishers, which may mean that the nets help them to find and catch fish that have been scattered in the confusion. In other words, both sides seem to gain something from the arrangement.",
      "Interestingly, not every dolphin in the area takes part. Only certain animals regularly work with the fishers, and researchers believe that young dolphins learn the behavior by following their mothers. The study also suggested that cooperative dolphins may survive longer than others, perhaps because they spend less time in parts of the lagoon where they risk being caught accidentally in illegal nets. If this is correct, the tradition is not merely a habit but a way of life that helps the animals stay alive.",
      "Yet the partnership may be in danger. The number of mullet has fallen in recent years, and fewer young people in Laguna want to become fishers when other jobs offer a more stable income. Changes in water quality and in the shape of the channels caused by human activity may also make the lagoon less suitable for the dolphins. The researchers warned that if either the dolphins or the people stop passing the practice on to the next generation, it could disappear, and it would not be easy to rebuild once the knowledge had been lost on both sides.",
      "Some scientists therefore argue that the Laguna tradition should be seen as a kind of cultural heritage shared by two species. Protecting it would mean not only managing fish stocks but also supporting the community that keeps the practice alive. As the case suggests, conservation is not always about separating humans from nature; sometimes it is about protecting the relationships that already exist between them.",
    ],
