    paragraphs: [
      "Anyone who lives with a cat has probably seen this strange behavior. The cat steps onto a soft blanket or a person's lap and begins to push its front paws up and down, one after the other, as if it were making bread. Many owners call this \"kneading,\" and some say the cat is \"making biscuits.\" The cat often closes its eyes and purrs while it does this, so it clearly seems to enjoy the activity. But why do cats do it at all?",
      "The most common explanation begins with kittens. When very young kittens drink milk from their mother, they press their paws against her body near the place where they feed. Kittens begin to do this within days of being born. Scientists believe that this pressing helps the milk to flow more easily. Since the action brings food, warmth and comfort, the kitten learns to connect it with the feeling of being safe. Many adult cats seem to keep this habit long after they have stopped drinking milk.",
      "This idea also explains why cats often knead when they are calm and relaxed. A cat on a warm lap may feel much as it did as a kitten beside its mother, so the old movement comes back. ( A ), some cats knead more when they are being stroked, and a few even suck on the blanket at the same time. In this sense, kneading may be a sign that the cat trusts the person it is sitting on. Not every cat kneads, however, and some do it much more often than others.",
      "There are other possible reasons as well. Cats have small scent glands between the soft pads of their feet. When they press their paws into something, they may leave a smell that people cannot notice but other cats can. Kneading could therefore be a quiet way of saying, \"This place is mine.\" Some researchers also think that wild cats long ago pressed down grass or leaves to make a soft and safe bed before lying down. Pet cats may still carry a small part of that old behavior. Scientists are still not sure which idea is right, and the answer may be a mix of them.",
      "For owners, kneading can sometimes be painful, because cats may push out their sharp claws as they press. Experts usually advise people not to punish the cat, since the behavior is natural and often shows that the cat is happy. Instead, owners can put a thick blanket on their lap or keep the cat's claws short. With a little care, kneading need not be a problem. Then both the cat and the human can enjoy a quiet and comfortable moment together.",
    ],
