    id: 'adv-traffic-cones',
    title: 'The Orange Cone on the Road',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Almost everyone has seen a traffic cone, but very few people have ever stopped to look at one carefully. These bright orange objects appear wherever there is roadwork, an accident, or a short-term change to the normal flow of cars. Because they are so common, we hardly notice them at all. Yet the simple cone is the result of many small decisions about safety, cost, and the way people pay attention.",
      "According to one popular story, the first cones were made in the United States in the early twentieth century by a man who painted lines on city streets. He wanted a way to mark the areas where the paint was still wet, so he made a set of heavy cones out of concrete. They did their job well, but they were hard to move, and they could badly damage a car that hit them. After the Second World War, makers began to use rubber and then plastic. These materials were much lighter and safer.",
      "The design of the modern cone is carefully thought out. Its orange color stands out against gray roads, green trees, and blue skies, so drivers can see it from far away during the day. At night, ( A ), bright color alone is not enough, and so many cones carry white bands that reflect the light from car headlights. The shape is important too. A wide, heavy base keeps the cone standing in strong wind, while the narrow top makes it less likely to roll far if it is knocked over.",
      "Another reason for the cone's success is that it is hollow. Workers can stack dozens of cones on top of each other, so a small truck can carry enough of them to close a whole lane of a highway. They are also cheap to make and easy to replace. When a car accidentally hits a plastic cone, the cone usually bends or falls over without causing much damage, and the driver is warned that something is wrong. In this way, the cone protects both the workers on the road and the people in the cars.",
      "Cones are now used in many places that have nothing to do with traffic. Sports coaches set them out on fields so that players can practice running and turning. Schools and shops use them to mark wet floors or closed areas. In the Scottish city of Glasgow, people have for many years placed a cone on the head of a statue of a famous soldier on a horse. The city council once suggested making the statue's base higher to stop the joke, but thousands of people objected, and the plan was dropped.",
      "The traffic cone shows that good design does not have to be complicated. A useful object must be easy to see, easy to carry, cheap, and safe, and the cone manages all of these things at once. The next time you pass one on the street, you might give it a second look. It is a small reminder that someone, somewhere, once thought hard about how to keep us safe.",
    ],
