    id: 'kyodai-on-swings',
    title: 'On Swings',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Of all the objects in a playground, the swing is perhaps the most puzzling, for it takes its rider nowhere. It carries a child forward and back along the same short arc, again and again, and when the ride is over the child steps off exactly where the ride began. Yet children will wait in line for a turn on a swing while slides and climbing frames stand empty, and many adults who pass an empty swing feel a brief, slightly embarrassed urge to sit down on it. Adults, too, often speak of the swings of their childhood with an affection they rarely show for any other piece of playground equipment. Something in that repetitive motion seems to answer a need that is not satisfied by getting anywhere.",
      "Part of the answer lies in the peculiar sensation at the top of each arc. For a fraction of a second, as the swing pauses before falling back, the rider seems to become weightless, suspended between rising and descending. It is a small taste of flight, but it is also a small taste of fear, since the body cannot quite believe that the chains will hold. Children often cry out at exactly this moment, half in delight and half in alarm. The pleasure of the swing depends on this mixture: a risk that feels real enough to excite us, contained within a structure that we trust will bring us safely back to where we started.",
      "Anyone who has learned to swing knows that strength alone achieves very little. A child who kicks wildly at the wrong moment merely jerks the seat and loses speed, while one who leans back and stretches the legs at just the right point in each arc rises higher and higher with surprisingly little effort. The secret is timing, and children learn it not from explanations but from the feeling of the swing itself, which rewards good timing and punishes bad timing at once. Small movements, repeated in harmony with the natural rhythm of the swing, accumulate into a motion far larger than any single push could produce, and it is hard not to see in this a lesson that applies well beyond the playground.",
      "Many of the achievements we admire in people's lives are built in this way. A language, a musical instrument, or a friendship rarely yields to a single heroic effort; it grows through modest actions performed regularly and at suitable moments. ( A ) trying to force progress with sudden bursts of energy, the skilled learner, like the skilled swinger, attends to rhythm, noticing when effort will be multiplied by circumstance and when it will merely be wasted. Impatience, which demands immediate results, is in this sense a kind of bad timing rather than an excess of commitment.",
      "There is also the matter of being pushed. Most of us first experienced a swing with someone standing behind us, whose hands sent us forward and then waited to meet us as we came back. Pushing a swing well is itself a small art, for the pusher must match the rhythm of the rider, applying force only at the moment the seat begins to move away. In this sense the push resembles a conversation more than a command: each contribution has to fit what the other person is already doing, and a push delivered at the wrong moment is not merely useless but can bring the whole motion to an awkward halt.",
      "Why, then, do most adults stop swinging? The obvious reason is that the equipment is designed for small bodies, but the deeper reason may be that adult life values motion only when it leads somewhere. We travel in order to arrive, work in order to earn, and exercise in order to improve our health; an activity whose only result is the pleasure of doing it can seem childish or even irresponsible. Perhaps this is why an adult who does sit on a swing tends to glance around first, as if making sure that no one is watching. The swing quietly challenges this assumption, reminding us that some experiences are valuable not as means to an end but simply because they are lived.",
      "Finally, every ride on a swing must end, and there are two ways of ending it. One may stop moving one's legs and allow the arcs to shrink gradually until the feet drag in the dust, or one may leap from the seat near the height of its swing and land some distance away. Children usually prefer the leap, and perhaps there is wisdom in both choices: the patience to let something come to rest in its own time, and the courage, when the moment is right, to jump toward whatever comes next.",
    ],
