    id: 'adv-recorded-voice',
    title: 'Why Does My Voice Sound Strange on a Recording?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Many people feel uncomfortable when they hear a recording of their own voice. They may ask, 'Do I really sound like that?' The voice on the recording often seems higher, thinner and less pleasant than the voice they hear when they are speaking. Friends and family, however, usually say that the recording sounds completely normal. Why is there such a difference between the voice we hear and the voice that other people hear?",
      "The answer lies in the way sound reaches our ears. When other people hear us speak, the sound of our voice travels through the air to their ears, and this is the only way they can hear it. When we hear ourselves, though, the sound arrives by two different routes. Part of it travels through the air from our mouth to our ears, just as it does for other people. Another part travels through the inside of our head.",
      "When we speak, our voice makes the bones and other parts of our head shake slightly. These small movements, called vibrations, carry sound directly to the inner ear. Sound that travels in this way tends to make low tones stronger. ( A ), the voice we hear while speaking seems deeper and fuller than the voice that travels only through the air. A recording, on the other hand, catches only the sound in the air, so it lacks the extra low tones that we are used to.",
      "This means that the recording is actually closer to the way other people hear us. In a sense, the voice we know best is one that nobody else can hear. Because we have heard our 'inside' voice all our lives, the recorded voice feels unfamiliar, and unfamiliar things can seem strange or even unpleasant at first. This may help to explain why so many people dislike their recorded voice, even when the people around them notice nothing unusual about it.",
      "The good news is that people can get used to their recorded voice. Singers, radio announcers and actors often listen to recordings of themselves to improve their performance, and over time the recorded voice stops sounding so strange to them. Language learners can also use recordings in this way. By recording themselves reading English aloud and then listening carefully, they can notice problems with their pronunciation and check how their voice sounds to listeners.",
      "So, if you are surprised by your voice in a video or a voice message, you do not need to worry. Your voice has not suddenly changed, and there is nothing wrong with it. You are simply hearing it from the outside, in the same way that everyone around you hears it every day.",
    ],
