    id: 'adv-why-squinting-helps',
    title: 'Why Squinting Helps Us See',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Have you ever tried to read a sign far away and found yourself narrowing your eyes? Many people who need glasses do this without thinking when they are not wearing them. Squinting really does make blurry things look clearer for a short time. But how can closing your eyes a little help you see better? The answer has to do with the way light enters the eye.",
      "When you look at something, light from it passes through the pupil, the small black opening in the middle of your eye. The lens inside the eye then bends this light so that it comes together on the retina at the back of the eye. If the light meets exactly on the retina, you see a sharp image. If your eye is not quite the right shape, however, the light meets a little in front of or behind the retina. Then each point of the object becomes a small, blurry circle instead of a clear point.",
      "The size of these blurry circles depends on how wide the opening is. Light enters through every part of the pupil, and the rays coming through the edges are the ones that miss the right spot by the most. When you squint, your eyelids cover the top and bottom of the pupil and leave only a narrow gap. Fewer rays from the edges can get in, so the blurry circles become smaller and the image becomes sharper. Your eyelids may also press gently on the eye and change its shape a little.",
      "The same idea is used in a pinhole camera, which can make a clear picture with no lens at all because its opening is extremely small. There are even special glasses covered with tiny holes that work in a similar way. Squinting makes blurry things look sharper. ( A ), it also makes things look darker, because less light can enter the eye. This is one reason why squinting works better on a bright day than at night.",
      "Squinting is a useful trick, but it is not a long-term answer. Keeping the muscles around your eyes tight for a long time can make them tired and may even give you a headache. If you often find yourself squinting to read the board at school or the signs on the street, it may be a sign that your eyes have changed. In that case, it is a good idea to have your eyes checked, so that you can see clearly without having to make a face.",
    ],
