    id: 'kyodai-on-cobwebs',
    title: 'On Cobwebs',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Every house has corners that escape the broom. High up where a wall meets the ceiling, behind a wardrobe, or in the angle of a window that is rarely opened, a few grey threads gather and slowly thicken into a cobweb. We notice them, usually, at the worst possible moment: when guests are about to arrive, or when the low winter sun slants across a room and reveals, with embarrassing clarity, everything we have failed to clean. A cobweb, more than almost any other household sight, seems to accuse us of neglect, and we reach for a duster with a faint sense of guilt.",
      "Yet the thing we call a cobweb is often not a working web at all. Many of the dusty tangles in our corners have been abandoned by the spiders that made them. What we see is less a trap than a ruin, a structure that has outlived its builder and slowly filled with dust. A web in a garden on a summer morning is a very different object. It is so fine that it is almost invisible, and we usually notice it only when dew settles on its threads and turns it into a pattern of shining beads. ( A ), the cobweb in a house becomes visible only because something has settled on it: not water, which vanishes by midday, but dust, which stays.",
      "This is why a cobweb is such a reliable measure of time. A table can be wiped in a moment, and a floor can be swept in the hour before the visitors come. But a cobweb takes weeks to thicken into a visible tangle and longer still to grow grey, and it appears only where no one has looked for a long while. It is, in a sense, a record of attention withdrawn. In a house that has been shut up for years, it is the cobwebs that tell us most clearly that no one has lived there, more clearly even than the silence or the smell of damp.",
      "Perhaps this explains why cobwebs appear so often in our images of the past. In paintings, novels and films, the forgotten attic and the deserted mansion are hung with them, and the word itself has come to describe whatever has been left unused for too long. We speak of clearing the cobwebs from our minds after a long illness or a dull winter, as though thoughts, too, could gather dust when they are left undisturbed. The cobweb has become a symbol not of the spider that made it but of the human beings who were absent while it grew.",
      "It is worth remembering, though, that every cobweb began as a remarkable piece of work. A small creature, without plans or tools, drew from its own body a thread that is light and, for its thickness, impressively strong, and arranged it into a structure suited to its purpose. That it did so in the corner of our living room rather than in a hedge does not make the achievement any less. We sweep away in a second what took patient hours to build, and we rarely give a thought to the builder.",
      "There is something in this contrast that reaches beyond spiders. Much of the work in the world resembles a cobweb in a corner: unseen while it is being done, noticed only once it has been abandoned, and remembered chiefly as evidence of neglect. The careful labour that keeps a building standing or a household running rarely attracts attention, while its absence becomes visible at once. We praise the clean room, if we praise anything at all, and not the many small acts of care that produced it and that must be repeated, unnoticed, every week.",
      "I do not suggest that we should leave our cobwebs where they are. A house in which no one ever cleaned would soon become uncomfortable, and some sense of shame at a dusty corner is probably healthy. But the next time the low sun reveals a grey thread hanging from the ceiling, it may be worth pausing for a moment before reaching for the broom. The cobweb is telling us two stories at once: one about the attention we failed to give, and one about the quiet work that went on regardless, in a place where no one thought to look.",
    ],
