    id: 'kyodai-on-wallpaper',
    title: 'On Wallpaper',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Of all the things in a home, wallpaper may be the one that is looked at least. When we first see it, perhaps in a shop or in a book of samples, we examine its pattern carefully, comparing colours and imagining how it will look in a particular room. Once it is on the wall, however, it quickly disappears from our attention. We sleep beside it, eat beside it and argue beside it for years, and if someone asked us to describe it with our eyes closed, many of us would struggle to do so with any accuracy.",
      "This disappearance is not a failure of the wallpaper but, in a sense, its purpose. A pattern on a wall is designed to be repeated, and repetition is precisely what the mind learns to ignore. A picture hung on the same wall invites us to stop and look, because it is single and has clear edges; wallpaper, by contrast, has no centre and no edge, and asks nothing of us at all. It belongs to the background, that part of our surroundings on which we rely precisely because we do not have to think about it.",
      "Yet the background is not unimportant merely because it goes unnoticed. A room with bare walls and the same room covered in a warm pattern can feel entirely different, even to a visitor who could not say what has changed. Wallpaper works on us in the way that the climate, the language we grew up with or the customs of our town work on us: steadily, and largely beneath the level of conscious attention. The things we stop seeing may shape our moods and expectations more than the things we deliberately look at.",
      "Wallpaper returns to our attention at two moments in particular. The first is when it is damaged, for a torn corner or a stain suddenly draws the eye to a surface that had been invisible for years. The second is when it is removed. Anyone who has stripped the walls of an old room has had the experience of finding, beneath the current paper, another pattern, and sometimes another beneath that, each chosen by someone who lived there before. ( A ), the wall turns out to be not a single surface but a series of layers, each recording the taste of a different time.",
      "These hidden layers are strangely moving. The people who chose them presumably expected their patterns to be admired, at least for a while, and then lived with them until they were no longer seen. Their choices were eventually covered by someone else's, not because anyone set out to erase them, but because tastes changed and the old pattern began to look tired and out of date. Much of ordinary life, it seems, is preserved in this way, not in museums or archives but under the newest layer of something that people have stopped noticing.",
      "There is a lesson here about how we treat what is familiar. We tend to value what is new and striking, and to assume that whatever we no longer notice no longer matters very much. Yet the people, routines and places that have faded into the background of our lives are often the ones on which everything else depends. We become aware of them, as we do of wallpaper, mainly when something goes wrong or when they are about to be taken away from us.",
      "It would be impossible, and probably exhausting, to look at everything around us with fresh eyes every day, since the mind must let some things recede in order to attend to others. But it may be worth, now and then, turning deliberately to the background, as one might stand in a familiar room and study the pattern on its walls. We might then ask what it has been doing for us during all the time that we were not looking at it.",
    ],
