    id: 'adv-kendama',
    title: 'Kendama: A Simple Toy That Rewards Practice',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A kendama looks like a very simple toy. It consists of a wooden handle with a point at one end and three cups of different sizes, together with a wooden ball that has a hole in it and is joined to the handle by a string. The player tries to catch the ball in one of the cups or on the point. Anyone can understand the goal in a few seconds, but learning to do it well can take months or even years of practice.",
      "Toys in which a ball must be caught in a cup or on a stick have been played with in many parts of the world for centuries. In France, a version called the bilboquet was popular as long ago as the sixteenth century, and similar toys were known in other regions as well. The kendama as it is known today, however, took shape in Japan in the early twentieth century, when a toy with side cups was designed and produced in Hiroshima Prefecture. The city of Hatsukaichi in that prefecture is often described as the birthplace of the modern kendama.",
      "The game became widely played in Japan, especially among children, and in the 1970s an association was formed to set standard rules and to hold competitions. Players can earn grades by performing a series of tricks successfully, in a way similar to the grade systems used in martial arts or calligraphy. This system gave learners clear goals to aim for, because once they had mastered one trick, there was always a harder one waiting for them.",
      "More recently, kendama has found many new fans outside Japan. Young people in North America and Europe began to perform complex tricks, often combining several moves in quick succession, and they shared videos of their performances online. ( A ), a traditional Japanese toy came to be seen in some countries as a kind of street sport, similar to skateboarding, with its own styles, competitions and well-known players. International competitions are now held, and some of them take place in Hatsukaichi itself.",
      "Why does such a simple toy attract so many people? One reason is that progress is easy to see. At first, a player may fail to catch the ball even once in the biggest cup, but after some practice the same move becomes easy, and new tricks become possible. Each success gives a small feeling of achievement, which encourages the player to keep trying. Another reason is that kendama requires balance and timing rather than strength, so people of different ages can enjoy it together.",
      "Some teachers and researchers have also become interested in kendama as a way of developing concentration and the coordination between the eyes and the hands. It is too early to say how large such benefits are, and more research is needed before firm conclusions can be drawn. Still, the popularity of kendama shows that a toy does not need electronic parts to hold people's attention. A ball, a string and a piece of wood can be enough, as long as there is always something new to learn.",
    ],
