    id: 'adv-ino-tadataka-map',
    title: 'Walking to Map a Nation: Ino Tadataka',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In the early nineteenth century, Japan acquired its first map of the whole country based on a careful, measured survey of its coastline. It was produced not by a professional scholar trained from youth but by a man who began serious study only after retiring from business. Ino Tadataka was born in 1745 in what is now Chiba Prefecture. As an adult, he managed the affairs of a wealthy merchant family in the town of Sawara, which dealt in rice and sake among other goods. He was by all accounts a capable businessman, but his deepest interest lay elsewhere, in the movements of the sun and the stars.",
      "At around the age of fifty, Ino handed the family business over to his son and moved to Edo, the city now called Tokyo. There he became a student of Takahashi Yoshitoki, an astronomer working for the shogunate who was about nineteen years younger than he was. Ino studied mathematics and astronomy intensely, observing the sky night after night. He became especially interested in a question that scholars of the time were debating: how long is one degree of latitude on the surface of the earth? Answering it would require measuring a long distance from north to south with great care.",
      "This ambition led to his famous surveys. In 1800, at the age of fifty-five, Ino obtained permission from the government to survey the route to Ezo, now Hokkaido, a region in which the shogunate had a strong interest. He and a small team walked the whole distance, measuring as they went. The results were so impressive that further surveys were authorised, and over the following years, until 1816, Ino and his team travelled along almost the entire coastline of Japan, as well as along many inland roads. In total, the routes they measured on foot are said to have amounted to roughly 35,000 kilometres.",
      "Their methods were simple but demanding. The team measured distances with ropes, chains and counted paces, and they recorded the direction of each section of the route with compass-like instruments. At night, whenever the weather allowed, Ino observed the positions of stars to work out the latitude of the places where they stopped, which allowed him to check and correct errors that had built up during the day. ( A ), the maps he produced were far more accurate than earlier maps of Japan, which had often been based on rough estimates and older documents.",
      "Ino did not live to see his work completed. He died in 1818, and his assistants, together with Takahashi Yoshitoki's son, finished the map and presented it to the shogunate in 1821. His death was reportedly kept secret until then so that the project could be completed under his name. The full set consisted of maps at three different scales, the largest of which covered the country on more than two hundred sheets. Ino's estimate of the length of one degree of latitude, about 28.2 ri, or roughly 111 kilometres, was also remarkably close to the modern value.",
      "Ino's story is often told as an example of starting late and achieving greatly. He began his second career at an age when many people of his time expected to rest, and he spent most of his last two decades walking and measuring in all kinds of weather. His maps remained important long after his death and were used as a basis for later maps of Japan. Today Ino is remembered not only as a mapmaker but also as a symbol of the idea that curiosity and persistence can flourish at any stage of life.",
    ],
    ja: [
      '19世紀のはじめ、日本は、海岸線を注意深く実際に測量したことにもとづく、初めての全国の地図を手に入れた。それをつくったのは、若いころから訓練を受けた専門の学者ではなく、商売から身を引いたあとになって本格的な勉強を始めた人物であった。伊能忠敬は1745年に現在の千葉県で生まれ、大人になってからは、佐原の町で米や酒などをあつかう裕福な商家の切り盛りをした。だれに聞いても有能な商人だったが、彼のいちばんの関心は別のところ、つまり太陽や星の動きにあった。',
      '50歳ごろ、伊能は家業を息子にゆずり、現在の東京である江戸へ移った。そこで彼は、幕府に仕える天文学者で、自分より19歳ほど年下の高橋至時の弟子になった。伊能は数学と天文学を熱心に学び、夜ごとに空を観測した。彼はとくに、当時の学者たちが議論していた一つの問いに関心をもつようになった。地球の表面で緯度1度の長さはどれくらいなのか、という問いである。それに答えるには、南北に長い距離をたいへん注意深く測る必要があった。',
      'この大きな志が、有名な測量へとつながった。1800年、55歳のとき、伊能は幕府から、幕府が強い関心をよせていた地域である蝦夷地、現在の北海道への道のりを測量する許可を得た。彼と小さな測量隊は、測りながら全行程を歩いた。その成果があまりにみごとだったので、さらなる測量が認められ、それから1816年までの年月に、伊能と測量隊は日本のほぼすべての海岸線と、多くの内陸の街道をたどった。合わせて、彼らが歩いて測量した道のりはおよそ3万5000キロメートルにのぼったと言われている。',
      '彼らの方法は単純だが骨の折れるものだった。測量隊は縄や鎖、数えた歩数で距離を測り、道のりの区間ごとの方角を方位磁石のような器具で記録した。夜には、天気が許すかぎり、伊能は星の位置を観測して、泊まった場所の緯度を割り出した。これによって、昼のあいだにたまった誤差を確かめて直すことができた。この注意深さのおかげで、彼のつくった地図は、大まかな見積もりや古い文書にもとづいていることの多かったそれ以前の日本地図よりも、はるかに正確だった。',
      '伊能は自分の仕事が完成するのを見ることなく、1818年に亡くなった。弟子たちが高橋至時の息子とともに地図を仕上げ、1821年に幕府に提出した。事業を彼の名で完成させるため、その死はそれまで伏せられていたと伝えられている。全体は三種類の縮尺の地図からなり、そのうちもっとも大きな縮尺のものは、200枚以上で国土をおおっていた。緯度1度の長さについての伊能の推定値、約28.2里、つまりおよそ111キロメートルも、現代の値に驚くほど近かった。',
      '伊能の物語は、遅く始めて大きなことをなしとげた例としてよく語られる。彼は、当時の多くの人が休むことを期待する年齢で第二の人生を始め、最後の20年間の大半を、どんな天気の中でも歩き、測ることに費やした。彼の地図は死後も長く重要でありつづけ、後の日本地図の土台として使われた。今日、伊能は地図の作り手としてだけでなく、好奇心とねばり強さは人生のどの段階でも花開きうるという考えの象徴としても記憶されている。',
    ],
