      "In the summer of 1545, an English warship called the Mary Rose sailed out to fight a French fleet near Portsmouth, on the south coast of England. The ship had served King Henry VIII for more than thirty years and was one of his favourite ships. But on 19 July, during the battle, it suddenly leaned over and sank. Hundreds of men were on board, and only a small number of them survived.",
      "Nobody knows exactly why the Mary Rose sank. Some people believe that the ship turned too sharply in a strong wind. Others think it was carrying too many soldiers and heavy guns. Many experts agree that water came in through the openings for the guns on the lower deck, which were probably open at the time. Once water entered these openings, the ship would have filled quickly. The full truth may never be known, but the question still interests historians today.",
      "For more than four hundred years, the ship lay on the bottom of the sea. Soft mud covered much of it, and this mud protected one side of the ship from the sea creatures that eat wood. In the 1960s, a team of divers began to search for the wreck, and they found it in 1971. Over the following years, divers brought up thousands of objects from inside the ship. ( A ), on 11 October 1982, the remaining part of the ship itself was lifted from the sea while millions of people watched on television.",
      "The objects found on the Mary Rose tell us a lot about everyday life at sea in the sixteenth century. Divers found longbows and arrows, cooking pots, shoes, combs and even dice used for games. They also found the tools of the ship's doctor and the bones of a small dog, which may have been kept to catch rats. Scientists have also studied the bones of the sailors to learn about their health, their work and the places they came from.",
      "Keeping the wooden ship from falling apart was a long and difficult job. For many years, the ship was sprayed with water and then with a special chemical that slowly took the place of water inside the wood. After that, the wood was carefully dried. The whole process took more than thirty years. Today, the Mary Rose and many of the objects found with it can be seen at a museum in Portsmouth, not far from the place where it sank almost five hundred years ago.",
