      "On the morning of December 6, 1917, the port city of Halifax in Canada was busy with ships. The First World War was still going on, and Halifax was an important harbour for ships carrying soldiers and supplies across the Atlantic Ocean. One of these ships was the Mont-Blanc, a French cargo ship. Its holds were full of explosives that were going to be used in the war in Europe.",
      "At about a quarter to nine, the Mont-Blanc was moving slowly through a narrow part of the harbour. At the same time, a Norwegian ship called the Imo was going the other way, out toward the sea. The two ships tried to avoid each other, but each misunderstood the other's signals and turns, and the Imo hit the front of the Mont-Blanc. The crash itself was not very strong, but it started a fire on the French ship.",
      "The crew of the Mont-Blanc knew what was in the holds, so they left the ship in small boats and rowed quickly to the other side of the harbour. The burning ship drifted toward the city and stopped next to a pier. Many people in Halifax did not know about the dangerous cargo. They came down to the shore or stood at their windows to watch the fire and the thick black smoke.",
      "At about five minutes past nine, the Mont-Blanc exploded. It was one of the largest explosions caused by humans before the age of nuclear weapons. Buildings across a large part of the city were destroyed, and a huge wave rushed over the shore. About 2,000 people were killed and around 9,000 were injured. Many of the injured were hurt by flying glass, ( A ) they had been looking out of their windows when the ship exploded.",
