    title: 'Why Jumping Beans Jump',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In some shops in Mexico and the United States, you can buy small brown \"beans\" that move by themselves. If you hold one in your warm hand or put it in the sun, it may suddenly roll over or even jump a little into the air. These are called Mexican jumping beans. They look like magic toys, but there is a simple and interesting reason why they move: a living insect is inside each one.",
      "First of all, jumping beans are not really beans. They are parts of the seed cases of a shrub that grows in the dry hills of northwestern Mexico. A small moth lays its eggs on the shrub's flowers or young seed cases. When a young insect, called a larva, comes out of its egg, it goes into a seed case and eats the seed inside. After that, the larva lives inside the empty case, protected by its hard shell.",
      "The larva moves to make the case jump. It holds onto silk threads that it has spun on the inside wall, and then it quickly throws its body against the shell. The case moves only a little each time, but the larva can repeat the movement many times. The beans move most when they become warm, and they become quiet when they are cool.",
      "Why does the larva do this? In the wild, the cases fall to the ground in hot weather, and direct sunlight can make them dangerously hot for the insect inside. Scientists think that the movements help the larva get out of the sun. One study found that the beans did not search for shade in a planned way. ( A ), they rolled about almost at random, and this was enough to bring many of them into the shade sooner or later.",
      "After several months, the larva gets ready for the next stage of its life. It cuts a small round door in the shell but leaves it closed. Then it spins a cocoon and becomes a pupa. Later, the adult moth pushes the door open and leaves the case. The moth lives only a short time, but it can lay eggs on the shrub, and the cycle begins again.",
      "So the next time you see a jumping bean, remember that its jumps are not a trick. They are the movements of a small insect that is trying to survive in a hot, dry land. Even a tiny animal can find a clever way to protect itself from danger.",
    ],
