      "In 1741, a Russian expedition led by the Danish-born navigator Vitus Bering was returning from Alaska when its ship was wrecked on an uninhabited island in the North Pacific. Bering himself died there that winter, and the island now bears his name. Among the survivors was a German naturalist, Georg Wilhelm Steller, who used the long months of waiting to study the island's wildlife. His most remarkable observations concerned an enormous marine animal that grazed in the shallow waters near the shore. Within about three decades of his description, the animal had disappeared from the Earth.",
      "The creature, now called Steller's sea cow, was a relative of the manatees and dugongs that still live in warmer seas, but it was far larger. Steller's descriptions and later estimates suggest that adults could reach eight metres or more in length and weigh several tonnes. The animals fed on kelp and other seaweed in shallow coastal waters, and they appear to have had no true teeth, grinding their food with hard plates instead. Steller noted that they were slow and showed little fear of people, and that they seemed unable to dive deeply, so their backs often remained above the water.",
      "These qualities made the sea cow easy to hunt. The shipwrecked sailors killed some for food and found the meat good and plentiful, since a single animal could feed many people for a long time. When the survivors finally built a small boat from the wreckage and returned to Kamchatka in 1742, they brought news not only of the sea cows but also of the island's sea otters and fur seals. That news drew fur hunters to the islands, and the hunters, ( A ) the sailors before them, relied on sea cows for meat during their long stays.",
      "The killing was wasteful as well as relentless. Accounts from the period suggest that many animals were wounded and escaped only to die at sea, so that far more were lost than were eaten. The population was probably already small when Steller saw it, perhaps numbering no more than a couple of thousand around two islands, and it could not withstand such pressure. The last reliable report of a sea cow being killed is generally dated to 1768, just twenty-seven years after the animal was first described by a scientist.",
      "Fossils show that the sea cow and its close relatives were once found much more widely around the edge of the North Pacific, from Japan to California. Why the species had already retreated to a few remote islands before 1741 is still debated. Some researchers believe that early human hunters reduced its range over thousands of years. Others point to the decline of sea otters, which eat sea urchins; where otters vanished, urchins may have multiplied and destroyed the kelp forests on which the sea cows depended. Both explanations may contain part of the truth.",
      "Steller's sea cow is often cited as a striking case of a large animal wiped out by people within a few decades of its scientific discovery. Its story is a reminder that rarity and vulnerability can go unnoticed until it is too late. The hunters who killed the last animals were not trying to destroy a species; they simply assumed that there would always be more. Today, when the remaining manatees and dugongs face threats of their own, the speed with which their giant cousin vanished serves as a warning that small populations can collapse very quickly.",
