    id: 'adv-computer-mouse-story',
    title: 'The Story of the Computer Mouse',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Today, many people use a computer mouse without thinking about it at all. They move it across a desk, and a small arrow moves across the screen at the same time. But this simple tool was once a surprising new idea, and its story began in the early 1960s, when computers were huge machines that most people never saw.",
      "At that time, Douglas Engelbart, an engineer at a research institute in California, was thinking about how computers could help people think and work together. He believed that people needed an easy way to point at things on a screen. With his colleague Bill English, who built the first model, he tried out several ideas. The first mouse was a small wooden box with two metal wheels underneath and a single button on top.",
      "Engelbart's team tested the new device against other tools, such as a light pen and a stick controlled by the hand, and the box with wheels worked best. Nobody is completely sure who first called it a 'mouse', but the name came from the wire coming out of the back, which looked like a tail. Whoever chose it, the name stuck, and people all over the world still use it today.",
      "In December 1968, Engelbart showed the mouse to an audience of about a thousand people at a computer conference in San Francisco. In the same presentation, he also showed text that could be linked and edited on a screen, and even a kind of video call with colleagues in another city. Many people who watched were amazed, and the event later became famous as one of the most important demonstrations in the history of computers.",
      "( A ), the mouse did not become common for many years. Computers were still too expensive for most homes and offices, and most people used them by typing commands. In the 1970s, researchers at another company in California improved the design, and in the 1980s personal computers made by Apple made the mouse familiar to ordinary users. After that, it quickly spread around the world.",
      "Interestingly, Engelbart himself earned very little money from his invention. The patent belonged to his institute, not to him, and he is said to have received nothing from it at all. Today many people use touch screens and touchpads instead. Even so, the basic idea that Engelbart had more than sixty years ago, pointing directly at what you want on a screen, is still at the center of how we use computers.",
    ],
