      "In the dry savannas of Africa, few trees are as easy to recognise as the baobab. Its trunk is enormously thick, sometimes wider than a small room, while its branches are short and twisted, and for much of the year they carry no leaves at all. Some traditional stories explain this odd appearance by saying that the tree was planted upside down, with its roots in the air. The baobab's strange form, however, is closely connected with the challenge of surviving long months without rain.",
      "Much of the explanation lies in the trunk. Unlike the hard wood of an oak, the wood of a baobab is soft and spongy, and it can hold large amounts of water. The trunk swells during the rainy season and shrinks as the dry months pass. By dropping its leaves during the driest part of the year, the baobab also reduces the amount of water it loses through evaporation. Scientists still debate how the stored water is used, and some suggest that it mainly keeps the trunk firm and helps the tree produce new leaves before the rains return.",
      "Most botanists recognise eight species of baobab. Six are native to the island of Madagascar, one is found across much of mainland Africa, and one grows in north-western Australia. How the trees came to be spread across such distant lands is still uncertain, but one suggestion is that their large, hard-shelled fruits floated across the ocean. The fruits contain a dry, powdery pulp that is rich in vitamin C, and people in many regions eat it or mix it with water to make a drink.",
      "Baobabs also support a wide range of animals. Their large white flowers open in the evening and last only about a day, and in mainland Africa they are pollinated mainly by fruit bats, which come to drink their nectar. ( A ), the leaves, bark and seeds provide food or shelter for insects, birds and mammals, and elephants sometimes tear into the soft trunks to reach the moisture inside during droughts. Many birds also build their nests among the thick branches or in holes in the trunk, where they are protected from the heat.",
      "Because the trunks of old baobabs are often hollow and made of several joined stems, their age cannot be found simply by counting rings. Scientists have therefore used radiocarbon dating, and they have found that some individual trees are more than two thousand years old. In 2018, however, a team of researchers reported that most of the oldest and largest African baobabs they had studied had died, or lost their oldest parts, during roughly the previous twelve years. The researchers suggested that changes in climate might be partly responsible, although the cause has not been proven.",
      "The baobab shows how closely a living thing can be shaped by its environment. Its thick trunk, its seasonal leaves and its relationships with bats and other animals all reflect the long rhythm of wet and dry seasons in which it evolved. If that rhythm changes, even a tree that has survived for thousands of years may find that its old ways of coping are no longer enough, which is why scientists are watching these giants with growing concern.",
