    id: 'adv-fermi-estimates',
    title: 'Clever Guesses: Fermi Problems',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "How many piano tuners work in a large city? At first, the question seems impossible to answer without a list of every tuner in the city. However, the Italian-born physicist Enrico Fermi, who won the Nobel Prize in 1938, is said to have enjoyed asking his students questions like this one. He believed that a good thinker could reach a sensible answer by breaking a big question into several small ones, and today such questions are often called \"Fermi problems\".",
      "Let us try the piano question for a city of about three million people. If an average home has three people living in it, the city has about one million homes. Perhaps one home in twenty has a piano, so the city may have about 50,000 pianos. If each piano is tuned about once a year, the city needs roughly 50,000 tunings every year.",
      "Next, we need to think about the tuners themselves. A tuner might be able to tune about four pianos a day and work about 250 days a year, which makes about 1,000 tunings for each tuner every year. If the city needs 50,000 tunings, it probably has about 50 piano tuners. This number may not be exactly right, but it is probably not as small as 5 or as large as 500.",
      "The surprising thing is that the small mistakes in such a calculation often balance each other. ( A ) one guess is too high, another may be too low, so the final answer is often closer to the truth than we expect. The most important skill, then, is not knowing the exact numbers but knowing which steps are needed and making each guess as reasonable as possible.",
      "Fermi problems are not just games for a classroom. Scientists use them to check whether the result of a long calculation makes sense, and engineers use them to make plans before they have exact data. Some companies even ask people who apply for jobs to estimate how many tennis balls would fit inside a bus. They do not expect a perfect answer, because what they really want to see is how the person thinks.",
      "You can practise this skill in your daily life as well. How much water does your family use in a week, and how many students in your country will start high school next year? Break each question into small steps, make a careful guess for each step, and you may be surprised at how much you can work out without looking anything up.",
    ],
