      "In the 1880s, the orange and lemon growers of southern California faced disaster. A small insect called the cottony cushion scale, which had arrived from Australia around 1868, was spreading rapidly through their orchards. The insects attach themselves to branches and leaves and feed on the sap of the trees, and each female produces a white, cotton-like mass that can hold hundreds of eggs. Badly infested trees weakened and stopped producing fruit, and some growers were burning their trees in the hope of stopping the pest.",
      "The chemical sprays of the time did little to control the scale, and the insect had few natural enemies in California. Entomologists noticed, however, that in Australia, where the scale had come from, it was not regarded as a serious pest. This suggested that something there was keeping its numbers down. Charles Valentine Riley, the chief entomologist of the United States Department of Agriculture, decided to send a collector to Australia to find out what it was. Without a new solution, the citrus industry of the region seemed likely to collapse.",
      "There was one difficulty. Congress had forbidden the department to spend money on foreign travel, so the trip was arranged through the State Department, which was sending a group of Americans to an international exhibition held in Melbourne in 1888. The collector, Albert Koebele, used the opportunity to search Australian gardens, and he soon found a small red and black ladybird beetle feeding on the scale insects. He sent several shipments of these beetles, now usually called vedalia beetles, back to California in late 1888 and early 1889.",
      "The results were remarkable. Only a few hundred beetles were released, but they multiplied quickly, and both the adults and their young fed almost entirely on the scale. Within about a year, the pest had been brought under control in many orchards around Los Angeles, and growers began passing beetles to one another. Citrus production soon recovered, and the cost of the whole project was tiny compared with the value of the crop it had saved.",
      "The vedalia beetle became the first great success of what is now called classical biological control. In this method, a pest that has arrived in a new region is controlled by introducing a natural enemy from its original home. Because the beetle fed so narrowly on the scale, it posed little threat to other insects. ( A ), the story also contained a warning. In the 1940s, when farmers began spraying the new insecticide DDT, the beetles were killed along with other insects, and the scale returned in large numbers until spraying practices were changed.",
      "Not every attempt to repeat this success has ended well. Some animals introduced to control pests have become pests themselves, with the cane toad in Australia being a famous example, and scientists today test possible control agents carefully before releasing them. Modern programs therefore look for enemies that attack only the target pest and leave other species alone, as the vedalia beetle did. The vedalia beetle shows what biological control can achieve when the enemy is highly specialized. It also reminds us that even a successful solution can be undone by a later decision made without considering it.",
