    id: 'adv-laugh-track',
    title: 'The Story of the Laugh Track',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "If you watch old American comedy shows on television, you will often hear people laughing after each joke, even though you cannot see anyone in the audience. This sound is called a laugh track. In some shows the laughter came from a real audience, but in many others much of it was added later by sound engineers. For many years, the laugh track was a normal part of television comedy, and it still raises an interesting question: does hearing other people laugh make something seem funnier?",
      "The laugh track became popular in the 1950s, when many comedy shows began to be filmed instead of performed live. Without an audience in the room, the jokes could feel strangely quiet. A sound engineer named Charles Douglass solved this problem with a special machine. People called it the \"laff box,\" and it held many recordings of real laughter, from small giggles to long, loud roars. Douglass could choose which kind of laugh to play and exactly when to play it.",
      "His machine was so useful that many shows used it, and Douglass kept the details of how it worked secret for years. Even shows that were recorded in front of real audiences sometimes used the box to make the laughter louder or to fill silent moments. This was called \"sweetening.\" Many viewers did not know how much of the laughter they heard was real.",
      "( A ), not everyone liked the laugh track. Some writers and actors felt that it told viewers when to laugh, as if they could not decide for themselves. Since around the 2000s, many popular comedy shows have been made without it. These shows are often filmed more like movies, and they let viewers find the jokes on their own.",
      "But does the laugh track actually work? In 2019, scientists in London tested this question. They asked people to rate how funny some very bad jokes were. When the jokes were followed by recorded laughter, people rated them as funnier. The effect was even stronger when the laughter was natural rather than forced. The scientists explained that laughter is a social signal, so when we hear others laugh, we feel invited to join in.",
      "So the laugh track may be more than a trick. People laugh much more often when they are with others than when they are alone, and a recorded audience may make watching at home feel a little less lonely. Whether you love it or hate it, the laugh track shows how strongly we are influenced by the people around us, even when those people are only sounds from a machine.",
    ],
