      "On the afternoon of November 30, 1954, a woman named Ann Hodges was taking a nap on the sofa in her home in Sylacauga, a small town in the American state of Alabama. It seemed an ordinary, quiet afternoon, and nothing appeared unusual. Suddenly, something crashed through the roof of the house, bounced off a large wooden radio and struck her on the hip. When she woke up, the room was full of dust, and a black stone about the size of a grapefruit was lying on the floor.",
      "At first, nobody knew what had happened. Some neighbors thought that a plane had crashed, and others feared some kind of attack. However, many people in the area had seen a bright light moving across the sky and heard loud bangs a few moments before. Scientists soon confirmed that the stone was a meteorite, a piece of rock from space that had survived its fall through the atmosphere. It weighed about four kilograms.",
      "Hodges was not seriously hurt, but she had a large, painful bruise on her hip and upper leg. Photographs of the bruise appeared in newspapers across the country, and she became famous almost overnight. Reporters and curious visitors crowded around her house for days. ( A ) the careful records made at the time, she is often described as the first person known for certain to have been hit by a meteorite. There are older reports of similar accidents, but they are much harder to confirm.",
      "The meteorite soon caused a quarrel. Hodges and her husband rented their house, and the owner of the building argued that the stone belonged to her because it had landed on her property. The owner took the matter to court, but in the end the two sides settled out of court, and Hodges paid the owner in order to keep the meteorite. Many people at the time believed that such a rare object could be worth a great deal of money. The couple hoped to sell it for a large sum, but by then public interest had faded, and no good offer came.",
      "In the end, Hodges gave the meteorite to the Alabama Museum of Natural History, where it can still be seen today. Scientists say that the chance of being struck by a meteorite is extremely small, because most of them fall into the ocean or onto empty land where nobody lives. In addition, many meteorites are small, and a large number of them are never found at all. That is why her story is still remembered, more than seventy years later, as one of the strangest accidents of the twentieth century.",
