      "In 1971, three astronauts flew to the Moon on the Apollo 14 mission. One of them, Stuart Roosa, carried an unusual item in his personal luggage: a small container holding about five hundred tree seeds. Before he became an astronaut, Roosa had worked as a smokejumper, a firefighter who jumps from planes by parachute to fight forest fires. The U.S. Forest Service asked him to take the seeds into space, and he agreed.",
      "The seeds came from five kinds of trees, including redwood, pine and sycamore. While his two crewmates walked on the surface, Roosa stayed in orbit around the Moon, and the seeds circled it with him. Scientists wanted to know whether a trip into space would change the way the seeds grew. This question mattered, because people were already dreaming of much longer journeys into space. After the astronauts returned, however, the container burst open during cleaning, and the seeds were mixed together. Some people feared that they could no longer be used.",
      "Fortunately, a Forest Service scientist decided to plant them anyway. Many of the seeds sprouted, and the young trees grew normally. ( A ) the scientists could see, they were no different from trees grown from seeds that had stayed on Earth. In 1976, when the United States celebrated two hundred years since its independence, many of these \"Moon trees\" were given to towns, schools and parks around the country. A few are also said to have been sent to other countries as gifts.",
