    id: 'kyodai-on-picture-frames',
    title: 'On Picture Frames',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Visitors to an art museum spend most of their time looking at paintings and almost none looking at what surrounds them. Yet nearly every painting on the walls is enclosed by a frame, and many of those frames were made with as much skill as the pictures themselves. Carved, gilded and sometimes far larger than the canvas they hold, they are seen by everyone and noticed by almost no one. This blindness is, in a sense, a sign of their success, for a frame that draws attention to itself has usually failed at its task.",
      "What, then, is that task? The most obvious answer is protection. A frame holds a canvas steady, guards its edges from knocks and, when glass is added, keeps out dust and curious fingers. But protection alone does not explain the care lavished on frames, nor the fact that we frame things, such as photographs of relatives, that are in little danger. The frame does something more interesting, drawing a line between the picture and the world and announcing that what lies inside should be looked at differently from what lies outside.",
      "Consider how differently we treat a scene through a window and the same scene in a painting. Outside the window, our eyes wander freely, and nothing tells us where to stop. Inside the frame, the edges have been chosen, and everything within them seems to have been arranged for our attention. In this way the frame turns an image into a statement. Even a photograph of an ordinary street, once framed and hung on a wall, invites a kind of attention that the street itself rarely receives from the people who walk along it.",
      "Frames also carry the tastes of their own time, and this creates a curious problem. Many old paintings now hang in frames chosen not by their artists but by later owners, dealers or museums, who replaced the originals to suit the fashion of the day or the decoration of a particular room. ( A ), a picture painted in one century may be seen through the eyes of another. Some museums have spent years searching for frames closer to those the artists would have known, believing that the wrong frame can quietly alter how a painting is understood.",
      "Not every artist has welcomed the frame. Some painters of the twentieth century left their canvases unframed or carried the paint around the edges. They wished their work to stand in the room as an object rather than to open like a window onto another world. Their refusal is revealing, because it shows that the frame was never neutral. To remove it is to make a claim about what a painting is, just as to add an elaborate gold one is to make a claim about how much the painting is worth.",
      "Outside museums, the frame plays a quieter role in ordinary homes. We frame diplomas, wedding photographs and children's drawings, and in doing so we decide which moments of a life deserve to be kept in view. A framed drawing by a six-year-old is no more skilful than the dozens left in a drawer, but it has been chosen, and the choosing gives it a dignity that the others lack. In this sense, framing is a form of praise, a way of saying that something is worth looking at again and again.",
      "Perhaps the deepest lesson of the frame is that attention needs boundaries. The world offers far more than any of us can look at, and we make sense of it only by marking off small portions and giving them our full regard. A frame does this for a picture, but we perform the same act whenever we pause to notice a face, a sentence or a patch of evening light on a wall. The frame in the museum is simply the most visible example of a habit without which we could hardly see anything at all.",
    ],
