    id: 'adv-buttered-toast-physics',
    title: 'Why Toast Lands Butter-Side Down',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Almost everyone has had this experience. You are carrying a slice of buttered toast to the table, it slips off the plate, and it lands on the floor with the butter facing down. Many people treat this as proof that the world is against them, and the event is often given as an example of Murphy's law, the joking rule that if anything can go wrong, it will. But is the toast really unlucky, or is there a scientific reason for what happens?",
      "In 1995, a British science writer named Robert Matthews decided to find out. He published a paper in a physics journal in which he studied how toast moves when it slides off a table or a plate. He showed that the butter itself has very little to do with the result. A thin layer of butter adds almost no weight, so it cannot pull the toast over by itself. ( A ), the answer lies in the way the toast starts to turn as it falls.",
      "When a slice of toast slides off the edge of a table, one end drops first while the other end is still resting on the edge. This makes the toast begin to rotate. Matthews calculated that, from the height of an ordinary table, the toast usually has time to turn only about halfway before it hits the floor. Since the buttered side normally starts facing up, a half turn leaves it facing down. In other words, the result depends mainly on the height of the table, not on bad luck.",
      "Matthews also suggested ways to save your breakfast. If you see the toast beginning to fall, you can quickly push it off the table with a fast sideways movement, so that it has less chance to rotate. Another, less practical, solution would be to eat at a much taller table, around three meters high, which would give the toast enough time to turn all the way over. He even argued, half seriously, that the height of tables is linked to the height of human beings, and so, in a sense, to the laws of physics.",
      "The paper was not just a joke. It used real physics to explain an everyday event, and it was later supported by experiments in which schoolchildren dropped toast thousands of times. The toast landed butter-side down more often than not. In 1996, Matthews received an Ig Nobel Prize, an award given for research that first makes people laugh and then makes them think. His work is a good example of how interesting science can be found even in a kitchen.",
    ],
