    id: 'kyodai-on-trays',
    title: 'On Trays',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A tray is a flat board with a low edge, and that description makes it sound like almost nothing at all. It has no lid, often no handle and no purpose of its own; it simply carries other things from one place to another. Yet a tray solves a problem that every household and every restaurant meets many times a day. Human beings have only two hands, while the things we want to carry, such as cups, plates, glasses and spoons, are many, small and easily dropped or spilled.",
      "The tray turns many objects into one. A waiter who could not possibly carry six glasses by holding each of them separately can carry all six on a tray with a single hand. The tray gathers them into one load whose weight can be balanced. In this sense the tray is a kind of temporary organization. For the length of a journey from kitchen to table, a collection of unrelated things becomes a unit, moving together and arriving together, before being scattered again once it has been set down.",
      "This gathering requires skill, and anyone who has watched an experienced waiter will have noticed how much. The heaviest items go near the centre, the tall ones are kept away from the edge, and the arm is held so that the tray stays level while the body twists through a crowded room. A beginner, by contrast, tends to stare at the glasses and walk stiffly, which is perhaps the surest way of spilling what is in them. The tray rewards those who trust it and look ahead, and it punishes those who keep their eyes fixed on what they carry.",
      "The low edge, which seems the least interesting part of a tray, is in fact what makes it useful. A perfectly flat board would let a cup slide off at the slightest tilt, while a deep box would make it awkward to lift things in and out. The edge is just high enough to prevent small accidents and just low enough not to get in the way of the hand. ( A ), the tray stands somewhere between a plate and a box, holding things securely without enclosing them.",
      "Trays also carry a social meaning that goes beyond their usefulness. Bringing breakfast on a tray to someone who is ill, or to a parent on a special morning, is a familiar gesture of care. Part of its meaning comes from the fact that the tray allows a whole meal to be brought to a person, rather than the person to the meal. In many traditions a tray is also used to present a gift, a cup of tea or a letter, as though the object deserved a small stage of its own. In such moments, the tray turns the simple act of handing something over into a modest ceremony.",
      "At the same time, trays are associated with places where people often eat with little choice. The plastic tray in a school canteen, a hospital ward or an aeroplane holds a meal divided into compartments, each with its fixed portion. Carrying such a tray slowly along a counter can feel more like being processed than being served. The same object that expresses care in one setting can express efficiency, or even indifference, in another. What changes is not the tray itself but who carries it, and for whom.",
      "Perhaps the tray deserves more attention precisely because it is so rarely the centre of attention. Its whole purpose is to let other things be noticed: the tea rather than the board beneath it, the meal rather than the way it arrived. Many of the most valuable people in any group play a similar part, quietly gathering what is scattered and carrying it to where it is needed, and receiving little credit once it has been set down. A good tray, like such a person, does its work best when nobody thinks about it at all.",
    ],
