    id: 'kyodai-on-gutters',
    title: 'On Gutters',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Most people look at a house and see its walls, windows, door and roof, but few notice the narrow channel of metal or plastic that runs along the lower edge of the roof. The gutter is one of the least admired parts of any building. It is rarely beautiful, it collects dead leaves and moss, and in ordinary speech the word usually appears as an image of failure, as when someone is said to have ended up in the gutter. Yet without it, many buildings would slowly be damaged by the very rain that their roofs are designed to shed.",
      "The gutter's task is to collect water and take it somewhere harmless. A roof gathers rain over a large area and concentrates it along its lowest edge, and if the water simply poured off at that point, it would fall in a heavy line close to the walls. Over many years, such water can soak into brickwork, wash away the soil around the foundations and leave dark stains that are hard to remove. The gutter catches the flow, carries it along a gentle slope to a downpipe, and sends it safely away from the building.",
      "That gentle slope is a quiet lesson in the value of small differences. A gutter that is perfectly level tends to let water sit in it, and standing water encourages rust, rot and the growth of small plants. A gutter that slopes too steeply, on the other hand, sends the water rushing toward one end, where it may overflow during a heavy storm. A good gutter falls by an amount so slight that a passer-by would never see it, and yet that tiny angle largely decides whether the whole system works or fails.",
      "Like many useful things, the gutter is noticed mainly when it stops working. A blocked gutter may go unnoticed for months during dry weather and then reveal itself suddenly in the first heavy rain, when water spills over its edge and splashes noisily onto the path below. Clearing it is a simple enough task, yet it is easy to postpone, ( A ) the problem cannot be seen on most days of the year. Many of the troubles of a building, and perhaps of a life, begin in this way, with small neglects that matter only on the worst day.",
      "There is also a certain pleasure in a gutter that works well. On a rainy night, the sound of water running steadily through a downpipe has a calming quality quite different from the irregular drip of a leak. It is the sound of a problem being dealt with, of water going where it was meant to go. Children standing in a doorway during a storm often watch the stream from a downpipe rush across the ground with fascination, as though they had discovered a small river that appears only when it rains.",
      "The gutter also reminds us that every building is engaged in a long negotiation with the weather. A roof cannot stop rain from falling, and a wall cannot stop water from seeking the lowest path. What a well-designed house does is not to defeat the rain but to guide it, accepting that water will arrive and arranging the route by which it will leave. Some old buildings made a feature of this necessity, with carved stone spouts that threw water clear of the walls, a number of them shaped like animals or monsters.",
      "Perhaps the gutter has something to teach those of us who prefer to be admired for what we build rather than for what we maintain. Much of the work that keeps a household, a school or a city running consists of guiding things that cannot be stopped, of keeping channels clear and making sure that what arrives in abundance can also flow away. Such work is seldom celebrated, and it tends to be noticed only when it has been neglected. The modest channel at the edge of the roof is a reminder that it is no less important for that.",
    ],
