      "Sailors crossing warm oceans have long reported a strange sight: small silver fish bursting out of the water and sailing through the air for many metres before dropping back into the sea. These are flying fish, a family of several dozen species found in tropical and subtropical waters around the world. Despite their name, they do not fly in the way that birds do, since they do not flap their fins to stay in the air. Instead, they glide, and they have become remarkably good at it.",
      "The key to their gliding lies in their unusually large pectoral fins, the pair of fins just behind the head. When a flying fish leaves the water, it spreads these fins wide, so that they work rather like the wings of a glider. Some species also have enlarged pelvic fins further back on the body, which give them a second pair of lifting surfaces and make them look as though they had four wings. Their bodies are long and smooth, a shape that helps them build up speed under the water before they launch themselves.",
      "The launch is perhaps the most impressive part. A flying fish swims rapidly toward the surface and breaks through it at a shallow angle. Its body then rises into the air, but the lower part of its tail often stays in the water for a moment, beating very quickly from side to side. ( A ), the fish can gain extra speed before it lifts off completely. Some fish touch the surface again with their tails during a glide and repeat this action, which can extend a flight considerably.",
      "How far can they travel? Glides of around fifty metres appear to be common, and much longer flights, sometimes said to reach several hundred metres when a fish touches down and takes off again, have also been reported. Exact figures vary from one source to another, partly because measuring a fish in flight over open water is far from easy. Most flights last only a few seconds, but in that time the fish may move well out of reach of whatever was chasing it.",
      "Escaping predators is generally thought to be the main reason for this behaviour. Under the surface, flying fish are hunted by larger, faster fish such as tuna and dolphinfish. By leaping into the air, they move into a space where those hunters cannot easily follow. The strategy is not completely safe, however, because seabirds sometimes catch them in the air, and a fish that escapes one danger may land close to another.",
      "Flying fish are also important to people. They are caught for food in several parts of the world, and in Barbados the fish is so closely associated with the island that it is widely regarded as a national symbol. In Japan, too, they are eaten in some regions and are used to make a kind of soup stock. Scientists, meanwhile, have studied the shape of their fins to understand how such a small animal can glide so efficiently, a reminder that creatures of the ocean may still have something to teach us about flight.",
