    title: 'Who Invented the High Five?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "When players score a goal, students pass an exam or friends share good news, they often raise their hands and slap them together above their heads. This gesture, called the high five in English, is now common in many countries, but it is surprisingly new. Most people who have studied its history believe that it became popular only in the late 1970s, and even so, nobody can say for certain who did it first.",
      "The most famous story comes from American baseball. According to this story, in October 1977 a player named Dusty Baker of the Los Angeles Dodgers hit a home run in the last game of the regular season. As he returned toward the bench, his teammate Glenn Burke, who was waiting for him, raised his hand high in the air. Baker did not know what else to do, so he slapped it. Many people call this the first high five, and the gesture soon spread among the team's players and fans.",
      "However, there is another story. Some people say that the high five was started by college basketball players at the University of Louisville at around the same time. One of the players, Wiley Brown, is said to have suggested to a teammate that they should slap hands up high instead of down low. ( A ) there are at least two stories, it is hard to know which one is true, and the gesture may well have appeared in more than one place.",
      "What is clear is that the high five spread very quickly. By the early 1980s it could be seen in many different sports, and it had also moved off the field into everyday life. Today it is used to celebrate small successes as well as large ones, and many parents teach it to young children. There is even a National High Five Day in the United States, held each year in April, when people are encouraged to give high fives to others.",
      "Why did the high five become so popular? One reason may be that it is simple and needs no words. Two people raise their hands, make a loud sound together, and share a feeling of success. Some studies of sports teams have even suggested that players who touch each other more often, through high fives and similar gestures, tend to cooperate better, although it is difficult to tell which comes first. Either way, the gesture shows how quickly a new custom can spread when it is fun to use.",
    ],
