    id: 'adv-piano-key-colours',
    title: 'Why Piano Keys Are Black and White',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A modern piano usually has 88 keys, and almost everyone can picture them: long white keys side by side, with shorter black keys placed between some of them in groups of two and three. The pattern is so familiar that it seems as if it must always have been this way. In fact, the colors of keyboard instruments have changed over the centuries, and for a long time many keyboards looked quite different.",
      "The arrangement of the keys follows the musical scale used in Western music. In each octave there are twelve notes. Seven of them are played on the white keys, and the other five, which fall between some of those notes, are played on the black keys. This group of twelve then repeats again and again, from the lowest notes on the left to the highest on the right. Because the black keys are raised and grouped in twos and threes, a player can find any note by touch, without needing to look down at the keyboard.",
      "Early keyboard instruments, however, did not always use these colors. On many harpsichords made in France and some other parts of Europe in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, the colors were reversed. The long keys were dark, and the short keys were light. Dark woods such as ebony were often used for the long keys, while light materials such as bone or ivory covered the short ones. To a pianist of today, such a keyboard might look almost like a photograph in negative.",
      "Why did the colors change? Nobody knows for sure, and several explanations have been suggested. One is cost. On the older keyboards, the expensive light material was needed only for the small number of short keys, and later changes in the supply and price of materials may have made the opposite arrangement more practical. Another is fashion, because a bright keyboard may have looked more attractive in a room lit by candles. ( A ), by the middle of the nineteenth century white long keys and black short keys had become usual on pianos.",
      "Today most piano keys are no longer covered with ivory. Because the international trade in elephant ivory has been strictly limited for decades in order to protect elephants, piano makers have turned to plastics and other materials. Some of these are designed to feel like ivory under the fingers. The colors, however, have stayed the same, and the familiar pattern of black and white remains one of the most recognizable designs in the world of music.",
    ],
