    title: 'Why Do Barbers Have Striped Poles?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In many countries, a barber's shop can be recognised from far away by the pole outside its door. The pole is usually a cylinder with red and white stripes, sometimes with blue as well, that wind around it in a spiral. In many shops it turns slowly, so that the stripes seem to climb endlessly upwards. Today the pole simply means that hair is cut inside, but the design has a much older and rather surprising history.",
      "In Europe during the Middle Ages, barbers did far more than cut hair and trim beards. They also pulled teeth, treated wounds and performed bloodletting, a treatment in which a small amount of blood was taken from a patient. At that time, many people believed that removing blood could cure a wide range of illnesses. Because barbers had sharp tools and steady hands, they were often called on to do this work, and some came to be known as barber-surgeons.",
      "According to a popular explanation, the pole is a reminder of this practice. During bloodletting, a patient would grip a staff tightly so that the veins in the arm stood out more clearly. Afterwards, bandages were washed and hung outside to dry, and they could become twisted around the pole by the wind. The red is said to represent blood and the white the bandages. ( A ) historians cannot be sure that this is exactly how the sign began, the link with bloodletting is widely accepted.",
      "Over time, the work of barbers and surgeons began to separate. In England, the two groups were joined in a single company in 1540, but the surgeons left to form their own organisation in 1745, as medicine became more scientific. Barbers gradually stopped performing medical treatments and concentrated on hair, yet they kept the striped pole. In the United States, blue was often added to the colours. Some say that this was done to match the national flag, while others say that the blue stands for veins, but neither explanation has been proved.",
      "Striped poles spread to many parts of the world, including Japan, where barbers began using them after the country opened to Western customs in the nineteenth century. In Japan, the poles are usually red, white and blue, and they remain a common sight outside barbers' shops. Many people who pass one today probably do not know that it once advertised a medical treatment. The pole is a good example of how a sign can survive long after the activity that gave it meaning has disappeared.",
    ],
