    id: 'kyodai-on-letterboxes',
    title: 'On Letterboxes',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A letterbox is a hole through which the outside world is allowed to reach us. In Britain, it is often a narrow slot in the front door, covered by a metal flap. In Japan and the United States, it is more often a box beside the gate or at the entrance of a building. Whatever its form, it does the same job. It lets things enter our homes without our being there to open the door. Letters, bills, newspapers and advertisements arrive while we are asleep or at work, and they wait for us patiently until we return.",
      "There are, in fact, two kinds of letterbox, and they face in opposite directions. One belongs to the home and receives; the other stands in the street and sends. The public post box is a strangely trusting object. People drop into it letters that may contain money, apologies or applications for jobs, and then walk away, confident that a stranger will collect them and carry them to the right place. In Britain, the novelist Anthony Trollope, who worked for the Post Office, is often credited with helping to introduce such boxes in the 1850s, and many of them are still painted a bright red.",
      "The letterbox at home is a place of waiting. Before e-mail and messaging, people checked it with real hope, expecting a reply from a friend, news from a distant relative or the result of an examination. The sound of letters falling through the slot could make a whole household hurry to the door. ( A ), the letterbox often held a mixture of pleasure and fear. An envelope might contain good news or bad, and until it was opened, both were possible. Even now, many people feel a small tension when they see an official-looking letter with their name on it.",
      "Today, most personal messages travel through phones rather than through the letterbox, and what remains is largely bills, notices and advertisements. Yet the letterbox has not lost its meaning. Parcels and handwritten cards still arrive, and because they are now rare, they carry more weight than before. A letter in a familiar hand, lying among a pile of advertising leaflets, stands out immediately. Someone has chosen paper, written words that cannot be deleted and paid to send them. That effort is part of the message, and the letterbox is where we first receive it.",
      "The letterbox also marks a boundary. Anything may be pushed through it, but nothing is meant to be taken out by someone outside. It is open in one direction only, which makes it a small model of how many people wish to deal with the world: willing to receive news and requests, but at a time of their own choosing. Some households put up signs refusing advertisements, as if to say that the opening in the door is an invitation only to certain visitors. The letterbox thus expresses both openness and control, two needs that every home must somehow balance.",
      "There is something touching, too, in the fact that a letterbox carries a name and a number. It tells the postman, and anyone else who passes, that someone lives here and can be reached. People who have no fixed address often find it difficult to receive official letters, to apply for work or to open a bank account. An address with a box to receive post is easy to take for granted. Yet it is one of the quiet foundations of belonging to a society, a small proof that one has a place in the world where messages can find one.",
      "The letterbox, then, is more than a container for paper. It is a point of contact between private life and the wider world, a place of hope and anxiety, and a sign that someone can be found. As fewer letters are written, it may seem to be losing its purpose. But perhaps it is better to say that it is changing its role. When almost everything can be sent instantly, the few things that still arrive through the slot in the door remind us that some messages are worth the time it takes to deliver them.",
    ],
