    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Among the papers my mother left behind was a letter I had written to her from a summer camp when I was nine. It had been folded into quarters, opened, and folded again so many times that the creases had become soft and almost transparent, and at the point where the two folds crossed there was a small hole. The words themselves were unremarkable, a list of meals and complaints about mosquitoes. What moved me was the creases, which told me something that the letter itself did not. Someone had taken it out and read it, not once, but many times over many years.",
      "A crease is a curious thing. When a sheet of paper is folded and pressed flat, the fibres along the line are bent sharply and some of them are broken, so that the paper can never fully return to its original state. We can smooth it out with our hands, but the line remains, and the paper will tend to fold again in the same place. In this sense paper remembers. Anyone who has tried to refold a large road map knows the consequence. The map wants to follow its old creases, and if we try to fold it in any other way, it stubbornly resists us.",
      "Teachers of origami pay great attention to this memory. A beginner tends to fold softly and hesitantly, but an experienced folder runs a fingernail along each fold to make a sharp crease, because a clear crease guides the next step. Some complicated models begin with a sheet that has been carefully creased in a pattern of lines and then unfolded again, so that the shape is already present in the paper before any real folding takes place. ( A ), the creases serve as a kind of plan, a set of instructions written into the sheet itself and waiting to be followed.",
      "Creases in clothing have a more complicated reputation. A crumpled shirt suggests carelessness, yet a sharp crease down the front of each trouser leg has long been taken as a sign of neatness. The fashion for pressed trouser creases is often said to have spread around the end of the nineteenth century. One popular story claims that it began when trousers that had been folded during shipment were worn without being smoothed. I do not know whether the story is true. But I like the idea that an accident of packing became a standard of elegance, so that people went on to create deliberately what had first appeared by chance.",
      "The origin of the English word crease is uncertain, though some dictionaries suggest a connection with crest, the ridge along the top of something. Whatever its source, the word has travelled into unexpected places. In cricket, the lines painted on the grass to show where the batter must stand are called creases, and a batter who is safely behind the line is said to be in the crease. There seems to me something appropriate in this use. A crease, whether on a playing field or on a sheet of paper, is a line that tells us where we belong.",
      "We also speak of creases in the human face, the lines that form at the corners of the eyes or across the forehead. A great deal of money is spent every year on removing them, as though they were stains to be ironed out of a shirt. Yet facial lines, like the creases in my letter, are records of repeated movement. The lines at the corners of the eyes are often formed by years of smiling, and those on the forehead by raising the eyebrows in surprise or worry. A face without any creases at all would be, in a sense, a face that had never been used.",
      "I have kept the letter, and I have not tried to flatten it. A crease is the trace of an action that was repeated until it left a mark, and in this way it closely resembles a habit. It makes the next fold easier, which is useful when the fold is a good one and troublesome when it is not. Some creases are worth reinforcing and others are worth smoothing out, and much of the work of living may consist in telling the two apart. The creases in my mother's letter, at least, are ones I would not remove for anything.",
    ],
