    id: 'adv-leavitt-cepheid-distances',
    title: 'Blinking Stars and the Size of the Universe: Henrietta Leavitt',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "At the beginning of the twentieth century, astronomers faced a problem that seems surprisingly basic today. They could measure the positions of stars with great precision, but they had almost no way of telling how far away most of them were. A faint star might be a dim one nearby or a brilliant one at an enormous distance, and nothing in its appearance seemed to settle the question. The key that eventually unlocked the problem was found not by a famous professor at a large telescope but by a woman who spent her working days examining photographs at a desk in Massachusetts.",
      "Henrietta Swan Leavitt was born in 1868 and joined the Harvard College Observatory in the 1890s, at first as an unpaid volunteer. The observatory employed a group of women, often called computers, to measure and catalogue the stars recorded on glass photographic plates. The work was demanding and poorly paid, and the women were rarely expected to draw conclusions of their own. Leavitt was assigned to study variable stars, whose brightness rises and falls over time. To find them, she compared plates of the same part of the sky taken on different nights, searching for points of light that had changed.",
      "Many of the plates she studied showed the Magellanic Clouds, two hazy patches of light visible from the Southern Hemisphere, which had been photographed at a station that the observatory operated in Peru. In these clouds she identified well over a thousand variable stars, and she noticed something remarkable about one particular type, now known as Cepheid variables. The brighter ones took longer to complete each cycle of brightening and fading. In 1912 she published a careful study of a small group of these stars in the Small Magellanic Cloud, showing that the link between brightness and period was regular enough to be drawn as a smooth line.",
      "The importance of this finding lay in a simple assumption. Because all the stars in the Small Magellanic Cloud are at roughly the same distance from the Earth, differences in how bright they appear must reflect real differences in how much light they give off. ( A ), the period of a Cepheid, which can be measured simply by watching the star over a number of nights, reveals its true brightness. Comparing that true brightness with how bright the star looks from the Earth then tells astronomers how far away it is. This works in much the same way that the dimness of a familiar lamp tells us roughly how distant it is at night.",
      "Her relationship by itself gave only relative distances, since no one yet knew exactly how far away the Small Magellanic Cloud was. Other astronomers soon worked to calibrate it by estimating the distances of a few Cepheids closer to home. The most dramatic use came in the 1920s, when Edwin Hubble identified Cepheids in the Andromeda Nebula, which many astronomers then believed to lie within our own galaxy. The distance he calculated placed it far outside the Milky Way, showing that it was a separate galaxy and that the universe was vastly larger than many had imagined.",
      "Leavitt did not live to see this. She died of cancer in 1921, at the age of fifty-three. According to a frequently told story, a Swedish mathematician wrote to Harvard a few years later to ask about her work because he wished to nominate her for the Nobel Prize, only to learn that she had died. Since Nobel Prizes are not normally awarded to people who have died, the honour never came. Today, however, the relationship she discovered is often called the Leavitt law, and Cepheid stars remain one of the most important tools that astronomers use to measure distances beyond our galaxy.",
    ],
