    title: 'Natto: The Sticky Beans of Japan',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Natto is one of the best-known traditional foods in Japan, and it is also one of the most surprising for visitors. It is made from soybeans, but it does not look or smell like ordinary cooked beans. When you stir it with chopsticks, long, thin threads appear and stretch between the beans and the bowl. Many people love its rich taste, while others cannot stand its strong smell. Few foods divide opinion so clearly, even among people who were born and raised in Japan.",
      "Natto is made through fermentation, a process in which tiny living things slowly change a food. First, the soybeans are soaked in water and steamed until they become soft. Then a kind of bacteria often called natto bacteria is added, and the beans are kept warm for about a day. As the bacteria grow, they break down part of the beans and produce the strong smell and the special flavor. The sticky threads are mostly made of a natural substance that the bacteria produce while they are growing.",
      "For a long time, natto was made by wrapping steamed soybeans in dry rice straw. People did not know why this method worked, but we now know that natto bacteria live naturally on straw. When the hot beans were wrapped in it, the bacteria moved onto the beans and began to grow. ( A ), the results were not always the same, because other kinds of bacteria also lived on the straw. In the early twentieth century, Japanese scientists succeeded in separating the natto bacteria and growing them on their own.",
      "This discovery made it possible to produce natto of steady quality in factories, and today most natto is sold in small containers instead of straw. Natto is also known as a healthy food. It contains a lot of protein, and it is especially rich in vitamin K, which helps blood to clot and is important for strong bones. However, patients who take a medicine called warfarin to keep their blood from clotting are usually told not to eat natto, because the vitamin K can make the medicine work less well.",
      "The popularity of natto has long been different from region to region within Japan. Surveys have often shown that people in eastern Japan, especially in the Tohoku and Kanto regions, eat much more natto than people in western Japan. In the past, many people in the Kansai area rarely ate it at all. The gap has become smaller in recent years, and companies have also developed types of natto with a milder smell for people who find the usual kind too strong.",
      "Outside Japan, natto is still unfamiliar to most people, and many visitors find it hard to eat at first. Yet interest in fermented foods has been growing around the world, and natto can now be found in some supermarkets in other countries. Whether people love it or hate it, natto shows how a simple bean, a little straw and some invisible bacteria can together create a food with a long history and a strong character.",
    ],
