    id: 'adv-bus-bunching',
    title: 'Why Buses Come in Pairs',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Almost everyone who uses buses has had the following experience: you wait at a stop for a long time, and then two or even three buses arrive one right after another. It is natural to think that the drivers are careless or that the bus company has made a poor timetable. In fact, this problem, which transport experts call bus bunching, can happen even when every driver is doing a good job.",
      "To see why, imagine a line of buses that leave the station every ten minutes. At first, the gaps between them are equal. Then one bus is delayed for a few minutes, perhaps by a red light or by a passenger who needs extra time to get on. Because of this delay, more people have gathered at the next stop by the time the bus arrives, so it takes longer for everyone to board. The bus falls further behind, and at each stop after that, the crowd waiting for it becomes even larger.",
      "Meanwhile, the bus that follows it experiences the opposite. Because the bus in front has just collected most of the passengers, there are few people waiting at each stop, and the second bus can move along quickly. Little by little, it catches up with the late bus, and in the end the two travel together. ( A ), a small delay does not stay small, because the way the system works makes it grow.",
      "Bus companies have tried several ways to deal with this problem. One common method is to make a bus that is running early wait for a short time at certain stops, so that the gap between it and the bus in front grows again. Some cities now use computers that watch the position of every bus and tell drivers when to slow down, rather than asking them to follow a fixed timetable. Another idea is to help passengers get on faster, for example by using IC cards instead of cash or by letting people enter through every door.",
      "None of these methods is perfect. When a bus waits at a stop, the passengers already on board may feel annoyed, since they want to reach their destination as soon as possible. Still, studies suggest that keeping the gaps between buses even can reduce the average waiting time for passengers, and it can also stop some buses from becoming extremely crowded while others are almost empty.",
      "Bus bunching is a good example of how a small problem can grow when many parts of a system affect each other. A similar pattern can be seen in other places, such as trains on a busy line or lifts in a tall building. The next time two buses arrive together, you may remember that the reason is not always a careless driver but the way the system itself works.",
    ],
