    id: 'adv-stadium-wave-physics',
    title: 'How a Wave Travels Around a Stadium',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Anyone who has watched a large football or baseball game on television has probably seen the crowd perform a wave. A group of spectators jumps to its feet with arms raised and then sits down again. The people beside them do the same a moment later, so that a ripple of movement seems to run around the stadium. In many countries this is known as the Mexican wave, a name that became popular after the football World Cup held in Mexico in 1986, although several places in North America claim to have started it earlier.",
      "The wave is a curious phenomenon because nobody directs it. There is no conductor, and most of the people who take part cannot see more than a small section of the crowd. Each person simply reacts to the neighbours, standing up when those nearby stand and sitting down shortly afterwards. In 2002 a small team of physicists set out to study this behaviour scientifically. They analysed video recordings of waves in large stadiums and compared what they saw with mathematical models that are usually used to describe very different systems.",
      "Their measurements revealed surprisingly regular patterns. The wave typically travelled at about twelve metres per second, which corresponds to roughly twenty seats every second, and it was usually between six and twelve metres wide. The researchers also found that, in the stadiums they studied, waves tended to move clockwise when viewed from above, although nobody has yet explained this tendency with certainty. Most remarkably, a wave rarely started unless a few dozen people stood up at almost the same moment, because a smaller group usually failed to persuade its neighbours to join in.",
      "To explain these patterns, the researchers compared the crowd to what scientists call an excitable medium. In such a system, each part can be in one of three states: ready to be activated, active, or resting for a while before it can be activated again. A forest fire behaves in this way, since a tree that has burned cannot burn again immediately, and so do the waves of electrical activity that pass through the muscle of the heart. ( A ), a spectator who has just stood up and sat down is briefly unwilling to stand again, which prevents the wave from moving backwards.",
      "This comparison is more than a curiosity. Because the movement of people in crowds follows rules that can be described mathematically, models of this kind may help organisers predict how excitement or alarm spreads through a large audience. A wave of standing and sitting is harmless, but the same process of copying one's neighbours can also drive more dangerous behaviour, such as a sudden rush towards an exit. Understanding how small groups trigger large movements could therefore help the designers of stadiums and concert halls to plan safer spaces and to manage crowds before problems develop.",
      "The wave also offers a lesson about collective behaviour in general. It looks like a single, carefully coordinated performance, yet it emerges from thousands of people each following a very simple rule and paying attention only to those nearby. Similar patterns can be seen in flocks of birds turning together in the sky or in fish moving as a single school. In each case, order arises without a leader, which reminds us that some of the most impressive things a group can do are not planned by anyone in particular.",
    ],
