    id: 'todai-kidney-sales',
    title: 'Should People Be Allowed to Sell a Kidney?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Most people are born with two kidneys but can live a normal life with only one. A healthy person can therefore give one away while still alive, and thousands do so every year, usually to a relative or close friend. Yet the number of patients who need a new kidney far exceeds the number available, and many wait years while depending on dialysis, a demanding treatment in which a machine filters the blood. This shortage has revived an old and uncomfortable question: should people be allowed to sell one of their kidneys?",
      "In almost every country, the answer given by the law is no. In the United States, for example, a law passed in 1984 made it a crime to buy or sell human organs for transplantation. International medical bodies have also condemned payment for organs, most notably in the Declaration of Istanbul, agreed by doctors and officials in 2008. One exception is often mentioned in these debates. Since the late 1980s, Iran has allowed unrelated living donors to receive payment under a system involving the state and charities. Its supporters claim that it greatly shortened waiting lists, although the evidence is disputed and many sellers appear to have been poor.",
      "Those who favour a regulated market begin with the patients. In the United States alone, roughly 90,000 people are on the waiting list for a kidney, and several thousand each year either die while waiting or become too ill to receive a transplant. If even a modest payment encouraged more people to donate, supporters argue, many of these lives could be saved. They also point out that surgeons and hospitals are paid for their part in a transplant, while the donor, who bears the greatest personal risk, is expected to give for nothing. Finally, they argue that adults should be free to make decisions about their own bodies, as they already do when they take on dangerous jobs for money.",
      "Opponents reply that a market in kidneys would draw its sellers overwhelmingly from the poor. A person who sells a kidney to pay off a debt is not making a free choice in any meaningful sense, they argue, ( A ) the pressure of poverty leaves little real alternative. Studies of illegal sellers in countries such as India and Pakistan have reported that many said their health had become worse afterwards, that many were still in debt a few years later, and that many regretted the decision. Opponents also fear that payment could reduce unpaid donation by relatives, since a family member might feel less obliged to give when a kidney could simply be bought.",
      "Between these positions are proposals that would reward donors without creating an open market. Several countries already reimburse living donors for travel, medical costs and lost earnings, on the principle that nobody should end up poorer for having given an organ. Some reformers would go further and offer a fixed benefit paid by the state rather than by the patient, such as lifelong health insurance, or a tax credit. Because the amount would be set by the government and the kidney would go to whoever was next on the waiting list, rich patients could not jump the queue, and donors could not be pressured into bargaining. Critics, however, regard even these measures as payment under another name.",
      "The deeper disagreement concerns what we owe to people in desperate circumstances. One view holds that forbidding a sale protects the poor from being exploited, while the other holds that it simply removes one of the few options they have and leaves their poverty untouched. Supporters of a market find it strange that a person may risk injury on a building site for a wage but may not accept a smaller, carefully monitored risk to save a life. Opponents answer that some things lose their value when they are bought and sold. A society in which the bodies of the poor serve as spare parts for the rich, they say, has lost something important, however good its intentions.",
      "In my view, an open market in kidneys would be a mistake, since it would place the heaviest burdens on those least able to refuse. Yet the present position is also hard to defend, because it asks donors to accept real costs while offering them nothing in return, and it allows a shortage to continue that costs thousands of lives. The sensible course is to remove every financial barrier to donation and compensate donors fully for lost income. At the same time, we should test carefully whether a fixed state benefit increases the number of donors without drawing them only from the poorest. Such an approach would treat donors with fairness rather than charity, while refusing to turn the human body into a commodity.",
    ],
