    id: 'adv-shark-skin-denticles',
    title: 'The Tiny Teeth on a Shark\'s Skin',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "If you stroke a shark from head to tail, its skin feels fairly smooth, but if you move your hand in the opposite direction, it feels almost as rough as sandpaper. In the past, dried shark skin is said to have been used for polishing wood in some parts of the world. The reason for this roughness lies in the unusual structure of the skin itself, which has interested both biologists who study sharks and engineers who design machines.",
      "A shark's skin is covered with thousands of tiny structures called dermal denticles, a name that means 'little skin teeth.' They are not scales of the kind found on most other fish. Instead, they are built in much the same way as our own teeth, with a hard outer layer and a softer inner part. Each denticle is usually less than a millimeter across, and in many fast-swimming species the denticles carry small ridges that run from front to back, along the direction in which water flows.",
      "Scientists have long suspected that these ridges help sharks swim efficiently. When an object moves through water, a thin layer of water close to its surface is slowed down, and small swirling movements in this layer produce a force called drag. The ridges are thought to keep some of these swirls under control, so that less energy is wasted. In one laboratory experiment, flexible pieces of real shark skin moved through water more efficiently with their denticles than after the denticles had been sanded away.",
      "This idea attracted the attention of engineers who wanted to reduce drag on vehicles. From the 1980s, researchers developed thin plastic films with tiny grooves, often called riblets, and tested them on aircraft. More recently, an airline group in Germany began covering parts of some of its planes with a film of this kind. It has reported that the film lowers fuel use by around one percent. That may sound small, ( A ) for a large number of planes flying every day, it can add up to thousands of tons of fuel each year.",
      "Shark skin also played a part in a well-known controversy in sport. Around the year 2000, a swimwear company introduced racing suits whose fabric was said to imitate the surface of a shark. Later, suits made of other high-tech materials helped swimmers set a remarkable number of world records. Partly because such results were seen as unfair, full-body suits of this type were banned in international competition from 2010. Interestingly, later research suggested that the sharklike surface of the fabric may have done little to reduce drag. Much of the benefit probably came from the way the suits pressed swimmers' bodies into a smoother shape.",
      "The story of shark skin shows both the promise and the difficulty of learning from nature. A structure that works well on a living animal, which can bend its body as it swims, does not always work in the same way when it is copied onto a stiff surface. Engineers who borrow ideas from nature therefore need to understand not only what a structure looks like but also how it behaves in its natural setting.",
    ],
