    id: 'adv-sea-salt-by-hand',
    title: 'Salt from the Sea, Made by Hand',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Salt is so cheap and easy to buy today that few people stop to think about where it comes from. In many countries, a large part of it is dug out of the ground as rock salt, which formed long ago when ancient seas dried up. Japan, however, has very little rock salt, and its wet climate makes it hard to dry seawater with sunshine alone. For this reason, people in Japan developed their own ways of making salt from the sea.",
      "The basic problem is that seawater is mostly water. Only about three percent of it is salt, ( A ) boiling seawater directly would use a huge amount of fuel. Salt makers therefore worked in two steps. First, they used the sun and the wind to make a strong salty liquid, called brine. Then they boiled only this brine, which needed far less wood than boiling ordinary seawater.",
      "One traditional method that is still used on the Noto Peninsula in Ishikawa Prefecture is called agehama. Workers carry seawater up from the shore in heavy buckets and spread it evenly over a flat field of sand. During the day, the sun and the wind dry the sand, and salt sticks to the tiny grains. The workers then collect the salty sand and pour more seawater over it, so that the salt washes out into a very strong brine.",
      "Along the calm coast of the Seto Inland Sea, a different method became common, especially from the Edo period. Large salt fields called irihama were built at the edge of the sea. The rise and fall of the tides brought seawater into them through channels, so workers did not have to carry it by hand. Towns such as Ako, in what is now Hyogo Prefecture, became famous for their salt.",
      "In the early 1970s, most of these salt fields disappeared. Japan changed to a factory method that uses electricity and special sheets called ion-exchange membranes to make strong brine quickly, without needing large areas of land. This method is efficient and does not depend on the weather, so it now produces most of the salt that is made in Japan.",
      "Even so, a few makers still use the old ways, and their salt is often sold as a special product. Some people say that it tastes softer, perhaps because it keeps small amounts of other minerals from the sea, although tastes differ from person to person. Either way, a pinch of hand-made salt reminds us how much work once went into something that we now take for granted.",
    ],
