    id: 'adv-guam-brown-tree-snake',
    title: 'The Snake That Silenced Guam\'s Forests',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "On many tropical islands, the forest is full of birdsong at dawn. On Guam, a small island in the western Pacific, it has been almost silent for decades. The reason is not hunting or the cutting of trees. Instead, it is a single animal, the brown tree snake, which arrived on the island by accident and went on to cause one of the best-known ecological disasters of modern times.",
      "The snake is native to parts of Australia, New Guinea, and nearby islands. Scientists believe that it reached Guam not long after the Second World War, most likely hidden in military cargo shipped from somewhere in that region. Because Guam had no native snakes of this kind, its birds, bats, and lizards had never needed to defend themselves against such a hunter, and they had no instinct to avoid it. The snakes are mainly active at night, and some grow to more than two metres in length, although most are a good deal smaller.",
      "With plenty of food and no natural enemies, the snakes multiplied quickly, and by the late 1960s they had spread across the whole island, climbing trees at night and taking eggs, chicks, and sleeping adult birds. ( A ) the efforts of wildlife officers, who tried hard to protect the last nests, most of Guam's native forest birds disappeared from the wild. Two species survived only because a few birds were taken into captivity, and others have been lost forever.",
      "The loss of the birds has changed the forest in ways that few people expected. Many of Guam's trees once depended on birds to eat their fruit and carry the seeds away, so fewer young trees now grow at a distance from their parent trees. Researchers have also reported that spiders have become far more common in Guam's forests than on nearby islands, perhaps because the birds that once ate them are gone. Scientists are still studying what other changes may follow from the loss of so many birds.",
      "The snakes cause problems for people as well. They climb electricity poles and lines, causing power cuts that can be costly for homes and businesses, and they sometimes enter houses. Some estimates suggest that these power cuts cost the island millions of dollars every year. There is also a fear that they could reach other islands, such as Hawaii, by hiding in cargo or aircraft, so trained dogs search outgoing goods for snakes before they leave Guam.",
      "In recent years, officials have tried new ways to reduce the number of snakes, including traps and dead mice containing a small amount of a common painkiller that is poisonous to the snakes. These mice are dropped into the forest from helicopters. Meanwhile, a bird called the Guam rail, which had disappeared from the wild, has been bred in captivity and released on a small nearby island without snakes. The story of Guam shows how a single unwanted passenger can change an entire island, and how difficult it is to undo such damage.",
    ],
