    title: 'Big Bodies, Few Tumours: The Puzzle of Peto\'s Paradox',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Cancer begins when a single cell acquires a set of genetic changes that allow it to divide out of control. Since such changes often arise as mistakes when cells copy their DNA, every cell division carries a small risk. It would seem to follow that an animal with more cells, and a longer life in which those cells divide, should face a much higher risk of cancer. A blue whale has vastly more cells than a mouse and may live for many decades, so it ought to be riddled with tumours. By the same reasoning, people should develop cancer far more often than mice, which have only a tiny fraction of our cells.",
      "In reality, this does not happen. Across different species, the rate of cancer does not appear to rise with body size or lifespan in anything like the way the simple calculation predicts. The British epidemiologist Richard Peto drew attention to this puzzle in the 1970s, and it has since become known as Peto's paradox. The paradox concerns comparisons between species, ( A ) within a single species the expected pattern can sometimes be seen; taller people, for example, appear to have a slightly higher risk of some cancers. Similarly, large breeds of dog are thought to be more prone to certain bone cancers than small breeds.",
      "Some of the clearest clues have come from elephants. Studies published around 2015 reported that elephants carry about twenty copies of a gene known as TP53, whereas humans have only one. This gene produces a protein that detects damage to DNA and, if the damage cannot be repaired, causes the cell to destroy itself. Elephant cells exposed to radiation in the laboratory were more likely than human cells to die rather than survive with damaged DNA. Researchers estimated that fewer than five per cent of elephants die of cancer, compared with a considerably higher proportion of humans.",
      "Other animals seem to have reached a similar result by different routes. The bowhead whale, which can live for more than two hundred years, does not appear to rely on extra copies of the same gene. Instead, some early research suggests that its cells may be unusually good at repairing broken DNA. The naked mole-rat, a small rodent with a lifespan of over thirty years, rarely develops cancer at all. Scientists have proposed several explanations, including an unusual substance in its tissues that may prevent cells from crowding together.",
      "Why should such defences exist? The answer appears to lie in natural selection. As the ancestors of elephants and whales grew larger and lived longer, individuals that were poorly protected against cancer would have been more likely to die before they had raised many young. Over many generations, those with better defences would have left more descendants. In other words, the very size of these animals created pressure for the evolution of stronger protection against the disease. Mathematical models suggest that even a modest change, such as requiring one more genetic error before a cell turns cancerous, could greatly reduce the risk in a large body.",
      "Peto's paradox has attracted the attention of medical researchers, who hope that the defences of large animals may suggest new ways to prevent or treat cancer in humans. The task is not simple, however. Defences against cancer may carry costs, and a body that destroys damaged cells too readily might lose healthy tissue faster or age in other ways. What works for an elephant cannot simply be copied into a person. Some scientists have also asked whether large animals benefit from a slower metabolism, which may cause less chemical damage to DNA, but this idea remains debated. Still, by asking why giants rarely die of cancer, scientists have found a new way of looking at a very old disease.",
    ],
