    paragraphs: [
      "London has stood beside the River Thames for about two thousand years, and the river has given the city water, trade and transport. Yet the same river can also be a danger. When strong winds push sea water up the river from the North Sea at the same time as a high tide, the water level can rise far above normal, and low parts of the city could be flooded.",
      "The danger became clear in 1953. That winter, a powerful storm combined with a very high tide and sent the sea flooding over the coasts of eastern England and the Netherlands. Several hundred people are thought to have died in England alone, and many more in the Netherlands. Central London escaped serious damage, but experts warned that the next flood might not stop before reaching the city.",
      "After years of discussion and planning, work began on a barrier across the river at Woolwich, in the east of London. It was in use by 1982 and was officially opened in 1984. The barrier is about five hundred metres wide and has ten steel gates, the largest of which are about as tall as a five-storey building. Between the gates stand piers with shining silver roofs, which make the barrier one of the most recognizable sights on the river.",
      "The way the gates work is simple but clever. Most of the time, they lie flat on the riverbed, so ships can pass over them without any trouble. ( A ) a dangerous tide is expected, the gates are turned upward until they form a solid wall of steel. The sea water is held back, and the river behind the barrier stays at a safe level until the tide falls again.",
      "In its early years the barrier was closed only a handful of times, but it has been used much more often since then. By now it has been closed more than two hundred times, according to the agency that runs it. Part of the reason is that sea levels are slowly rising, and the land in the south-east of England is also sinking very slightly.",
      "The barrier was first designed to protect London until around 2030. Engineers now believe that, with careful repairs and upgrades, it can probably keep working until about 2070. After that, the city may need a new barrier or other ways to deal with higher seas. For now, though, the shining silver roofs remain a quiet reminder that a great river can be both a friend and a threat.",
    ],
