    id: 'adv-konigsberg-seven-bridges',
    title: 'Seven Bridges and a Puzzle With No Answer',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In the eighteenth century, the city of Konigsberg was an important port in Prussia, on the southern coast of the Baltic Sea. A river called the Pregel flowed through the middle of the city, and in the river there were two islands. Seven bridges connected the islands with each other and with the two banks of the river.",
      "The people of the city enjoyed walking on Sunday afternoons, and over time a popular puzzle grew up around these walks. Was it possible to take a walk through the city that crossed every one of the seven bridges exactly once? Many people tried, and some may have spent whole afternoons trying, but nobody seemed to succeed. Yet nobody could explain why it was so difficult, either, and the question remained a mystery.",
      "In 1736 the Swiss mathematician Leonhard Euler, who was working in Russia at the time, decided to study the problem. He realized that the shapes of the streets, the length of the bridges and the size of the islands did not matter at all. What mattered was only which pieces of land were connected and how many bridges joined them. This way of ignoring details in order to see the basic structure of a problem would later become very important in mathematics.",
      "So Euler reduced the city to four pieces of land and seven connections. Then he thought about a walker passing through any piece of land. Each time the walker arrives by one bridge, he must leave by another, so the bridges at that place are used in pairs. ( A ), a place touched by an odd number of bridges can only be the start or the end of the walk.",
      "In Konigsberg, every one of the four pieces of land was reached by an odd number of bridges. A walk can have only one start and one end, so at most two places could have an odd number. Euler therefore proved that the walk was impossible. He had not just failed to find an answer; he had shown that no answer existed.",
      "Today, many people see Euler's work as the beginning of a branch of mathematics called graph theory, which studies points and the lines between them. The same ideas are now used to plan delivery routes and computer networks, and even to study how diseases or rumours spread among groups of people. The city itself has changed, too: it is now called Kaliningrad, and only two of the bridges from Euler's time still stand.",
    ],
