    id: 'adv-extended-mind-notebook',
    title: 'Is Your Notebook Part of Your Mind?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Where does the mind end? For most people, the answer seems obvious: the mind is inside the head, and everything outside the skin, including pens, notebooks and phones, belongs to the outside world. In 1998, however, the philosophers Andy Clark and David Chalmers published a short paper that questioned this assumption. They argued that, under certain conditions, objects outside the body can become genuine parts of a person's thinking, an idea they called the extended mind. The paper was fairly short, but it has since become one of the most widely discussed works in the philosophy of mind.",
      "To make their case, they described two imaginary people. Inga hears that there is an interesting exhibition at a museum in New York. She thinks for a moment, remembers that the museum is on 53rd Street, and sets off. Otto suffers from a condition that damages his memory, so he carries a notebook everywhere and writes down any new information he learns. When he hears about the exhibition, he opens his notebook, finds the address, and sets off as well. He uses the notebook so constantly that it plays the same role for him that biological memory plays for Inga.",
      "Clark and Chalmers asked what the difference between the two cases really is. In both, a piece of stored information guides the person's behaviour in the same way. Inga's belief about the address was in her memory before she consulted it; Otto's was in his notebook before he consulted it. ( A ), it seems reasonable to say that Otto believed the museum was on 53rd Street even before he opened the notebook, just as Inga did before she remembered.",
      "The heart of their argument is sometimes called the parity principle. If some process in the world works in a way that we would call thinking when it happens inside the head, we should count it as thinking wherever it happens. That, at least, is what they suggested. The location of a process, in other words, should not decide whether it is mental. What matters is the role it plays in guiding thought and action.",
      "The authors did not claim that every object we touch becomes part of our minds. They proposed several conditions. The resource should be reliably available and regularly used; the information should be accepted more or less automatically, without constant doubt; and it should be easy to reach when needed. They also suggested that it should have been consciously accepted at some point in the past, although they treated this last condition as less certain. Otto's notebook meets these conditions, but a book in a library that someone happens to read once does not. These limits were meant to prevent the idea from spreading to almost everything.",
      "Not everyone was persuaded. Critics such as Fred Adams and Kenneth Aizawa argued that Clark and Chalmers had confused being connected to a mental process with being part of one. A pencil may be closely linked to the act of doing arithmetic, they pointed out, but that does not make the pencil itself a thinker. Others noted that memories in the brain change and blend over time in ways that words in a notebook do not, which might suggest a real difference between the two.",
      "The debate has become more practical since the paper was written. Many people now rely on smartphones to store phone numbers, plans and photographs, and some feel almost lost without them. Some researchers have even begun to ask whether depending on such devices changes the way our own memories work. If the extended mind view is right, then damaging or taking away someone's phone might be closer to harming the person than to damaging their property. Whether or not one accepts the theory, it raises a question that seems likely to grow more important: how much of who we are is now stored outside ourselves?",
    ],
