    paragraphs: [
      "The colander in my kitchen is a plain metal bowl, dented on one side, with a pattern of small holes punched through its base and walls. It hangs on a hook above the cooker, where it catches the light in the evening, and I take it down several times a week to drain pasta, rinse vegetables or wash rice. It is one of those objects that nobody ever praises, yet I would notice its absence immediately, and I have come to think that its quiet work contains a lesson worth considering.",
      "The purpose of a colander is, in a sense, a contradiction. It is a container designed to fail at containing, a bowl whose whole value lies in the holes that prevent it from holding water. Most vessels in a kitchen are judged by how well they keep things in; the colander is judged by how well it lets things go. Its maker must decide exactly how large the holes should be, for if they are too large, the food escapes with the water, and if they are too small, the water stays behind with the food.",
      "I learned to appreciate this when I first lived alone and owned no colander at all. For several weeks I drained my pasta by holding the lid of a saucepan against its edge and tipping the water into the sink. This method burned my fingers and frequently sent half my dinner after the water. When I finally bought a colander, the relief was out of all proportion to its price. It taught me that the simplest tools often solve problems we did not know we had until we struggled without them.",
      "There is a particular moment that I have always enjoyed. When a pot of boiling pasta is poured into the colander, a great cloud of steam rises from the sink and fogs the window, and for a second or two the kitchen disappears. My glasses, if I am wearing them, usually fog over as well, so that I stand almost blind for a moment in my own kitchen. Then the cloud thins, the water drains away, and what remains is exactly what I wanted, separated cleanly from what I did not. The whole process takes only a few seconds, but it has the satisfying neatness of a problem being solved in front of one's eyes.",
      "It seems to me that much of life involves a similar kind of sorting. We are constantly receiving information, opinions, tasks and memories, and we cannot possibly keep all of them. Wisdom may consist less in collecting things than in knowing what to let pass through. A mind without holes, like a bowl without holes, would soon be heavy with water and of little use. The difficulty, of course, is that people, unlike colanders, must decide for themselves what size of hole is right, and they often get it wrong.",
      "Some people, I think, have holes that are too small. They hold on to every slight they have suffered and every worry they have ever had, until their minds are full of water that should long ago have drained away. Others have holes that are too large, and let slip lessons and friendships that they would have done well to keep. Most of us probably move between the two, holding on too tightly in some matters and too loosely in others. A good colander, by contrast, keeps its shape and does its job without ( A ).",
      "I do not wish to make too much of a kitchen utensil, and I am aware that a colander does not think about anything at all. Yet the objects we use every day can sometimes show us ideas more clearly than books can, because we hold them in our hands and watch them work. When I take my dented colander down from its hook, I am reminded that letting go is not the same as losing. Sometimes it is precisely what allows us to keep the things that matter.",
    ],
