      "Kiruna, the northernmost town in Sweden, lies well north of the Arctic Circle, where the sun does not set for weeks in summer and hardly rises in the middle of winter. The town owes its existence to iron. Beneath it lies an enormous body of iron ore, and the mine that digs it out is often described as the largest underground iron ore mine in the world. For more than a century, the mine has provided a large share of the town's jobs.",
      "The problem is that the ore body runs at an angle under the town itself. As miners follow it deeper and deeper, the rock above the empty spaces gradually breaks and sinks. Cracks have appeared in the ground closer and closer to the old centre, and engineers concluded some years ago that large parts of the town would eventually become unsafe. The choice was either to stop mining, which would have badly damaged the local economy, or to move the town. Some houses close to the mine have already been left empty, and certain roads have been closed for safety reasons.",
      "The town decided to move. The project, which began in the early 2000s and is expected to continue for decades, involves building a new centre a few kilometres to the east and moving thousands of residents into new homes. Many ordinary buildings are simply being knocked down and replaced, and the mining company, which is owned by the Swedish state, pays much of the cost. ( A ), some buildings that people regard as especially valuable are being lifted and carried to the new site in one piece.",
      "The most famous example is Kiruna Church, a large wooden church built in the early twentieth century that was once voted the most beautiful building in Sweden. In 2025 it was reportedly placed on huge wheeled platforms and moved slowly along a specially widened road to its new location. Moving the church in one piece was a technical challenge, since the building is tall and its wooden frame could be damaged by sudden shaking. The journey was about five kilometres long and took around two days, and thousands of people are said to have gathered to watch it pass.",
      "Not everyone has welcomed the changes. Some older residents feel sad about losing streets and buildings full of memories. In addition, some members of the Sami community, the indigenous people of the region, have worried about the effect of mining on the land used by their reindeer. Others point out that the identity of the town has always been tied to the mine, and that moving it is a way of making sure that the community can survive at all.",
      "Kiruna is not the only town in the world that has had to deal with the effects of mining beneath it, but the scale of its move is unusual. Planners hope that the new centre will be more compact, easier to heat in the long winters and more pleasant to walk around. Whether the new town will feel like home to those who move there, however, is a question that only time can answer.",
