      "Nauru is a tiny island in the middle of the Pacific Ocean, a little south of the equator. With an area of only about twenty-one square kilometres, it is one of the smallest countries in the world, and its population is thought to be only around twelve thousand. You can drive all the way around it in less than half an hour. Yet for a short time in the twentieth century, this small island was famous for being remarkably rich.",
      "The source of that wealth lay under the ground. Much of the island was covered with a thick layer of phosphate, a valuable material used to make fertilizer for farms. Scientists believe that it built up over millions of years on top of the ancient coral rock that forms the island. Around 1900, a visitor is said to have noticed that a piece of rock from Nauru was rich in phosphate, and mining began a few years later.",
      "For decades, the mining was controlled by foreign companies and governments, and most of the profit left the island. ( A ), after Nauru became independent in 1968, its people soon took control of the industry for themselves. Money poured in. By some estimates, income per person in the 1970s and 1980s was among the highest in the world, and the government provided free housing, schooling and medical care for its citizens.",
      "This success, however, came at a heavy cost. To reach the phosphate, miners removed the soil and plants from most of the central part of the island, leaving behind a strange landscape of sharp grey coral pillars. Today, roughly four-fifths of the island is said to be unsuitable for farming or housing, so most people live on a narrow strip of land along the coast.",
      "Daily life changed as well. With so much money and so little farmland, many families stopped growing and catching their own food and began buying imported products such as tinned meat, white rice and sweet drinks. Health experts have linked this change in diet to the very high rates of diabetes and other illnesses that are now reported on the island.",
