      "In the mid-1970s, an American microbiologist named Richard Blakemore was looking at mud from a marsh under a microscope when he noticed something odd. Certain bacteria in the sample all swam towards the same side of the drop of water. When he moved a magnet near the slide, the bacteria turned and swam in a new direction. He had found what are now called magnetotactic bacteria, tiny living things whose movement is guided by magnetic fields.",
      "Blakemore was not, in fact, the first person to see them. An Italian researcher, Salvatore Bellini, had described similar behaviour in the 1960s, but his reports did not appear in a scientific journal at the time and were little known outside Italy. Discoveries of this kind sometimes have to be made twice before the wider scientific community notices them. Today, however, Bellini's earlier observations are widely recognized, and historians of science often mention both names when they tell the story of how these unusual organisms came to be known.",
      "Inside each of these bacteria is a chain of very small crystals, usually made of a magnetic iron mineral called magnetite. Some kinds use a related iron mineral that contains sulfur instead of oxygen. Each crystal is wrapped in a thin membrane, and together the crystals work like the needle of a compass. The bacterium does not need to sense the field in any active way. The chain simply lines up with the Earth's magnetic field, and the whole cell is turned in that direction as it swims.",
      "Why would a bacterium need a compass? The answer seems to lie in the fact that the Earth's magnetic field is not horizontal in most places. In the Northern Hemisphere, it points down into the ground as well as towards the north. Most of these bacteria prefer places with little oxygen, which are usually found below the surface of the mud. ( A ), by following the field, they can move fairly directly down towards the conditions they need, instead of wandering about in three dimensions.",
      "An interesting observation supports this explanation. Many magnetotactic bacteria from the Southern Hemisphere tend to swim towards the south instead of the north. In that half of the world the field points upwards, so moving south still carries the bacteria down into the mud. This difference fits the idea that the main purpose of the compass is to help the bacteria find the right depth, not to guide them towards a particular place on a map.",
