    id: 'kyodai-on-bird-feeders',
    title: 'On Bird Feeders',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Outside my kitchen window there hangs a small wooden feeder, shaped like a house with a sloping roof and an open front. I bought it on a cold afternoon several winters ago, partly because I wanted something to look at while I washed the dishes. Each morning I fill its tray with seeds, and within a few minutes the first visitors arrive. Usually they are sparrows, quarrelling noisily over the best places, but sometimes a tit or a finch slips in among them, takes a single seed and is gone before I can really see it.",
      "For the first few weeks I simply enjoyed the movement. Gradually, however, I began to notice that the birds were not a crowd but a society, with habits and rules that I had never suspected. The sparrows came in groups and left in groups. The tits seemed to prefer to visit alone, waiting on a branch until the feeder was quiet. One bird, which I came to recognise by a pale mark on its wing, always arrived last and always ate longest, as though it had learned that patience brought its own rewards.",
      "I also learned that my small kindness was not as simple as I had imagined. A feeder that is not cleaned regularly can apparently help diseases to spread, since many birds touch the same tray and the same seeds within a single day. Some researchers have even suggested that garden feeding may be changing the habits of certain birds, so that some birds now spend the winter in places where food is easy to find instead of flying on. I do not know how far such findings apply to my own garden, but they made me look at the feeder differently.",
      "It is tempting, when we feed wild creatures, to imagine that we are their benefactors. The birds, of course, have no such idea. They do not thank me, and they do not seem to recognise me at all. When I open the back door, they scatter just as they would for a cat or a gust of wind. If the feeder were empty for a week, they would simply look for food elsewhere, and I suspect that most of them would survive perfectly well. The relationship is far more important to me than it is to them.",
      "Perhaps that is precisely its value. Much of what we do in daily life is done in the hope of being noticed, thanked or rewarded. Filling the feeder offers none of these things. It is a small act whose results I can watch but cannot claim, since the birds come for the seeds and not for the person who provided them. In this sense the feeder has taught me something about giving that I had not quite understood before: that a gift may be ( A ) even when the receiver has no idea who the giver is.",
      "The feeder has also changed the way I notice time. In winter the birds come early and stay late, and the tray is empty by noon. In spring their visits become shorter and more urgent, and I sometimes see a parent carrying seeds away to a nest that I will never find. By summer there are young birds with untidy feathers, clumsy at first and then suddenly confident. The calendar on my wall tells me the date, but the feeder tells me where I am in the year in a way that feels more alive.",
      "I sometimes wonder what the birds would make of my side of the window, if they thought about it at all. To them I am probably no more than a shape that moves behind the glass, of no greater interest than a cloud. Yet every morning, as I tip the seeds into the tray and step back inside, I feel that I have been allowed into a world that does not need me. That feeling, more than any sense of having done good, is what keeps me filling the feeder, winter after winter.",
    ],
