    paragraphs: [
      "In ponds, ditches and wet ground across much of the world grows a plant that most people would never notice. It has no proper roots, and its thin, feathery stems simply float in the water or spread through damp soil. Along these stems, however, hang hundreds of tiny bags, most of them no more than a few millimetres long. These bags give the plant its common name, the bladderwort, and they are among the fastest traps known in the living world. There are reported to be more than two hundred species, and some of them grow on land in wet moss or soil.",
      "Each bag works like a small vacuum. Glands in its walls are thought to pump water out of the bag, slowly lowering the pressure inside until the walls are pulled inward and the bag becomes slightly flattened. The entrance is closed by a flexible door that fits so tightly that water can hardly leak back in. At this stage the trap is set, holding a store of energy in its bent walls, rather like a rubber ball that has been squeezed and is waiting to spring back into shape. Setting the trap in this way is thought to take some time, but releasing it takes almost none.",
      "Near the door stand a few fine hairs. When a small water animal, such as a water flea, brushes against one of them, the door suddenly buckles inward and the walls spring outward. Water rushes into the bag, carrying the animal with it, and the door snaps shut again behind it. Researchers who have filmed the process with high-speed cameras report that the whole event can be over in about a thousandth of a second or less, far too quickly for the human eye to follow. Even a fast-swimming animal has almost no chance to react.",
      "Once inside, the prey cannot escape. The plant is thought to release substances that break down the animal's body, and the nutrients are absorbed through the walls of the bag. The glands then begin pumping water out again, and within an hour or so the trap is reported to be ready for another catch. Because the plant grows in water that is often poor in nutrients such as nitrogen, this steady supply of small animals may help it to grow where many other plants struggle.",
      "The trap was not always understood in this way. In the nineteenth century, Charles Darwin is said to have studied bladderworts closely. He appears to have believed, however, that animals forced their way in through the door, which then closed behind them like a one-way gate. Only later did scientists show that the plant itself actively sucks its prey inside. This discovery changed the bladderwort from a passive container into one of the most remarkable examples of rapid movement in any plant.",
      "More recent studies have added a further ( A ). Some traps seem to fire even when no animal has touched them, perhaps because the pressure inside slowly builds until the door can no longer hold. Such traps take in water containing algae, bacteria and small particles rather than animals. Some researchers suggest that these accidental meals may also provide the plant with useful nutrients, so that the bladderwort may not depend entirely on hunting, although this idea is still being discussed.",
      "The bladderwort is a reminder that speed in nature does not always require muscles or nerves. The plant has no nerves at all, yet it responds to touch faster than many animals do. By storing energy slowly and releasing it suddenly, a plant without a single moving joint can perform one of the quickest actions in biology. Some engineers who design tiny pumps and valves have shown interest in such traps, since they work underwater without any external power. Yet for most of us, the plant remains invisible, quietly catching its prey in a muddy ditch while we walk past without a glance.",
    ],
