    id: 'kyodai-on-clipboards',
    title: 'On Clipboards',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A clipboard is nothing more than a flat board with a spring clip at the top, and yet it has a curious power over the people who see it. Hand one to a stranger, together with a pen, and he or she seems at once to become an official of some kind. People step aside for the person with the clipboard, answer his or her questions and assume that he or she has a right to be wherever he is standing. I have often wondered how such a plain object came to carry so much authority.",
      "Part of the answer, I think, lies in what the clipboard allows its owner to do. It makes it possible to write while standing up, away from any desk. For this reason it belongs to people whose work takes them out of the office: inspectors, nurses on their rounds, coaches at the side of a field, people counting birds or traffic. The clipboard announces that its holder is not merely passing through but is there to observe, to check and to record. It is, in a sense, a portable desk that turns any place into a place of work.",
      "The authority of the clipboard is, of course, partly borrowed. There are well-known stories, some of them perhaps exaggerated, of people who walked into buildings where they had no business simply by carrying a clipboard and looking busy. Whether or not these stories are true, they are believed because they match our experience. We tend to judge a person's purpose by the objects he or she holds, and a clipboard suggests a purpose so clearly that we rarely think of asking what that purpose actually is.",
      "Being on the other side of the clipboard is a different matter. Anyone who has watched an inspector tick boxes while walking through a kitchen knows the feeling, and so does anyone who has watched a doctor make notes at the end of a hospital bed. It is the slight unease of being observed by someone whose writing one cannot see. The board is usually held at an angle that hides its contents. Its holder knows what is being written, and the person being written about does not, which gives even the most friendly conversation a faint flavour of examination.",
      "Yet I do not think the clipboard is only an instrument of power. Teachers on school trips carry them to make sure that no child has been left behind, and volunteers at a charity run use them to record the names of those who have finished. In such hands the clipboard is a tool of care, a way of making sure that nobody is forgotten. A name missing from such a list is a reason to go back and look again. The lists it holds are often lists of people, and to be on a list can mean being ( A ) as well as being checked.",
      "In recent years the clipboard has begun to give way to the tablet computer, which can hold thousands of forms and send them instantly to an office. The change is sensible, and I would not wish to stop it. Still, something seems to be lost. A tablet looks like the same device that we use for games and messages, and it does not carry quite the same air of quiet purpose. Nor does it leave behind the sheet of paper, crossed and annotated, that shows at a glance how a long day's work has gone.",
      "Perhaps what I value most in the clipboard is its honesty. It does not pretend to be anything more than a support for paper, and the authority people give it comes from the work that it makes possible rather than from any cleverness of its own. A board, a clip and a few sheets are enough to let a person stand in a field or a corridor and pay careful attention to what is in front of him or her. That kind of attention, it seems to me, deserves a little respect.",
    ],
