    paragraphs: [
      "Most people picture a spider sitting in the middle of a web, waiting for an insect to fly into it. Many spiders, however, never build such webs at all. Trapdoor spiders, which live in warm regions in many parts of the world, spend most of their lives in a burrow in the ground. The entrance to the burrow is closed by a small round door, and when the door is shut, it can be so well hidden that a person may walk past it without noticing anything.",
      "The spider builds its home with great care. It digs a tube in the soil with spines on its mouthparts, and then it lines the walls with silk, which keeps the burrow from falling in and may also help to keep out water. The door is made of soil, pieces of plant and silk, and it is attached to the top of the tube by a hinge of silk. Because its upper surface is covered with the same material as the ground around it, the door blends into its surroundings. Building a burrow and its door takes a great deal of time and energy, so the spider rarely abandons it.",
      "The spider usually hunts at night, and its method is patience. It waits just behind the door, which it may hold slightly open with its front legs. When a beetle, an ant or some other small animal walks nearby, the spider is thought to sense the vibrations of its footsteps. Some species are said to lay lines of silk on the ground around the entrance, which act like alarm wires. In an instant, the spider throws open the door, seizes the prey and pulls it down into the burrow.",
      "The door also protects the spider from its enemies. If something tries to open it from outside, the spider may hold it shut from below, gripping the underside with its fangs and legs. In some species the door is thick and fits tightly, like a cork in a bottle. Observers have reported that it can be surprisingly difficult to lift the door against the spider's grip. Not every enemy is kept out, however. Certain wasps are believed to specialise in hunting such spiders, and they may dig their way in or wait for the spider to ( A ) the door.",
      "Living in a burrow can bring a long and settled life. Many trapdoor spiders stay in the same burrow for years, making it wider as they grow. Females in particular may live much longer than people expect. One female studied in Western Australia was reportedly about forty-three years old when she died, which would make her one of the oldest spiders ever recorded. Her long life is thought to owe something to the safety of her home. Males, by contrast, usually leave their burrows when they become adults in order to search for females, and they tend to live much shorter lives.",
      "The young spiders face a difficult journey before they can build homes of their own. After hatching, they stay with their mother for a while, then leave to find a suitable place to dig. Many of them do not travel far, so that the burrows of a single family may be found close together. Because they move so little, some species are found only in a very small area, and scientists worry that such spiders may disappear if their habitat is destroyed.",
    ],
